        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The King and Queen at Sandringham.—Tbe King and Queen will this month entertain a succession of shoot- ing parties at Sandringham, where his Majesty invari- ably celebrates his birthday. The Queen seldom, if ever, walks out with the guns to watch the sport, but her Majesty and Princess Victoria usually join the luncheon party when the weather is fine, and sometimes the Princess will remain with the Royal party until the return for five o'clock tea, which, because of its dainty feasting and informal character,is always a pleasant snd a,greeable meal at Sandringham. The Queen and Princess Victoria will spend the greater part of the winter in , Norfolk, partly because her Majesty has made it her especial charge to look after her grandchildren whilst the Prince and Princess of Wales are away. Of all places too, the Queen likes Sandringham Palace. It is also always possible to get out at Sandringham, so long as it is fine overhead. The walks through the grounds have all been most carefully laid, and may be traversed on the wettest day with perfect cleanliness and comfort. Royal Pets.—Dogs and household pets abound at Sandringham, and there is one secluded spot in the grounds where many which have lived their alloted span, lie buried, and have a tablet erected to their memory. Most of the Royal favourites too have their pictures painted, and Miss Maud Earl, the well-known artist, tells in one of the November journals, the diffi- culties she has in obtaining good sittings from the animals. Horses are not so bad, but dogs she says, are the most tiresome sitters, and can never be induced to keep quiet. As a rule the dogs are brought to her studio, in charge of someone they are in the habit of minding, but, says Miss Earl, they are all bad sitters, and seeing that they can take no interest in the pro- ceedings, it is not surprising there is difficulty in keep- ing them still for so many hours as are required for the painting. The Duchess of Devonshire.—The health of the Duchess of Devonshire, is causing her numerous friends and relatives some anxiety. She did not derive so much benefit as was expected from takimg the baths and waters at Aux-les-13ains, in the summer, and was far from well when she went to Newmarket. Since then, the Duchess has had to cancel many of her social engagenients on account of her continued ill-health. She must necessarily somewhat feel the weight of advancing years, for she was married as far back as 1852, though the Duchess has always made a point of studying her health, and is aflrm believer in the benefits of out-door exercise. Of recent years she has done a good deal of tricyclin,g when in the country, and in London has usually taken a daily constitutional walk. For over half a century, the Duchess has been one of the most popular leaders of Society, first as the wife of the seventh Duke of Manchester and secondly, after left a widow in 1890, she married two years later the present Duke of Devonshire. The Roosevelt Family.—The world hears much more about Miss Alice Roosevelt than of Mrs. Roosevelt, the wife and daughter of the 'United States President. Miss Roosevelt has just returned home, after a three months visit to Japan, China, and other parts in the East, and it is expected that she will pay a visit to this country next spring. She is the step-daughter of the present Mrs. Roosevelt, who however was the Presi- dent's first fancy. The latter was a Miss Carow of New York, where the Roosevelt's and Carow's were regarded as two of the best families and an intimate friendship sprang up between the young people. Theodore Roosevelt however left New York, to go to college, and afterwards became engaged to, and married Miss Alice Lee, of Boston. About a year later she died, leaving a little daughter—Miss Roosevelt—who is named after her. —Subsequently Mr. Roosevelt again met the young girl with whom he had been such friends in his early days, and eventually married her, as his second wife. She is the present Mrs. Roosevelt, but though extremely able and clever, she has none of the American trait of thrusting her own personality forward, and does not aspire to any great social brilliance. She goes about her many duties, quietly and with as little display as possible. Miss Roosevelt on the other band, is a high-spirited girl, with a desire to see and know the world, and is much more a public woman than is the President's wife. Old Hallowe'en Customs.—ln the days of the" good old customs" there were many quaint observances associated with the 31st October, or All Saints' Eve. They were mostly remnants of heathen superstitions and credulous practices, by which it was sought to divine the future, and some of them which degenerated into mere merry-making still survive. The use of nuts in these various divinations led to its being called Nut- crack Night, a name still familiar in many rural parts of the country. A favourite custom was to place two nuts, named after two young people supposed to be in love with one another, on a bar of the flregrate, and see how they burned. If they remained close together it was a sign that all would go well, but if they started apart then it was not likely that it would come to a wedding. There were scores of similar divining customs, since gone out of date, by which young people sought to learn fate, but this one practised on Nutcrack Night would seem to have had as much merit in it as any of them. Fashionable Card Games.—lt is rumoured that Bridge is to be moved from the stalls to the upper boxes, as it were, in the theatre of fashionable card games, and that Poker is to take its place. Mrs. Arthur Wilson who claims to have been the first to introduce this game to the "Smart set" is a keen Poker player, and the same may be said of Lady de Trafford, and Mrs. Cavendish Bentnok. That Bridge will be altogether banished how- ever is unlikely in the extreme. Like the old-world whist, it will remain, which is as it should be, for the game teaches one to think, a process good for us all. White Headgear.—Last winter, several well-known Society women noted for their perfect taste in dress, pinned their faith to white headgear, and this season the idea has "caught on "to such an extent, that every other woman one meets is wearing either a white toque or hat. This is a state of affairs to be deplored, because white headgear suits only the pronounced blonde, or a decided brunette, and even then, like the Society women who started the mode, one needs an excellent</p>
        <p>piexion, and somewhat vivid coloring, to look one's 4st in an all-white hat or toque. Well and carefully chosen colors are better, and then there is the always smart blaek hat, than which little in the way of headgear looks better. Just now the cache-peignes are higher than ever, but these are so delightfully filled up with chiffon, wings, and feathers, that all sense of the ridiculous is banished, and the effect of the excessive tilt is smart in the extreme.</p>
        <p>New Colours.—Peacock bine is to enjoy a great vogue for evening frocks, so say authorities on the sub- ject, and another much to be sought after here is one I which goes by the name of" soap-suds." This is of an opalescent character, a pearly grey, and a delicate green, on a foam-like surface. Those women who possess sables will do well to select some of the new olive greens upon which this beautiful fur looks particularly well; and the latest violets and wine shades all promise to prove very modish, and most becoming. Much white and cream, but very little black, is being worn at the moment, indeed not for several years has the latter been so severely passed by. About Furs.—To name the favourite fur of the moment, would indeed be a difficult task, there are so many favourites. Among the more costly varieties, however, sable holds its own as ever, next perhaps comes chinchilla, and then white fox, and ermine. For the long three-quarter coat, sealskin is being largely employed, while broad-tail, and black Persian lamb is utilised for the same purpose, for those among us whose dress allowance does not run to sealskin. To enumerate all the furs bearing high-sounding names, yet whose origin is of the humblest, would be an impossible task, but suflice it to say that many of them are not to be despised. MADGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <p>mcDouGALL's Pateo SELF-RAISING FLOUR ALWAYS I T Riga .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>FOR OUR FAIR READERS.</head>
        <p>FOR OUR FAIR READERS.</p>
        <p>About Women.</p>
        <p>The Empress of Russia spends no less than £2,000 yearly on perfumes, soaps, and toilet waters, ordered almost entirely from Paris. The Empress of Germany is passionately fond of all kinds of flowers, but for some time has favoured orchids, of which she possesses a great variety. Lady Haldon has publicly acknowledged the great benefits she has derived from Dr. Williams' pink pills far pale people. "My heart became seriously affecte3," said her ladyship. " Symptoms of advanced anemia showed, with most acute indigestion. Food caused me such misery that I practically starved myself. Mv whole system was deranged, and I could not sleep. I gave a trial to Dr. Williams' pink pills, and within three days was astonished at the marvellous improvement. Very soon I was delighted to find my complaint had vanished. My general health was such that I had not enjoyed for many years. Madame Nellie Cope, the gifted soprano, has now so many pupils for singing that she seldom accepts other engagements. She once had other reasons for her refusals. " For years," she states," I suffered so from excessive weakness and anternia that any exertion was impossible. I had fearful headaches, feelings of sick- ness, and often gssped for breath. But a nurse advised me take Dr. Williams' pink pills, and I can truthfully say these pills make new, rich blood. I have no head- aches now ; and to all anremic girls I would say, Dr. oink pills are the only perfect medicine I know." Madame Neuie.. Cops address is 11, Cricket- field-road, Clapton, N.E. Not only for women, but for men also, are Dr. Williams' pink pills valuable. This has been proved by countless instances of cures published in the news- papers. These pills have cured antemia, indigestion, palpitations, consumption, eczema and skin disorders, neuralgia, St. Vitus' dance, rheumatism, sciatica, paralysis, locomotor ataxy, and ladies' ailments. But only the genuine ',ills cure—those labelled in full, "Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people." Substitutes are useless; in case of doubt send direct to Dr. Williams' medicine co. Holborn-viaduct, London, enclosing two shillings ancininepence for one box, or thirteen and nine for six boxes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>CAKEWALK. –</head>
        <p>THE CAKEWALK. -</p>
        <p>Application. Refused at Widnes.</p>
        <p>At Widn.E4, on Monday, Mr. Robert Kiddie, manager for " A Chinese Idyll " company appearing in Widnes this week, made an application for sanction for two children eleven and twelve years of age to perform. Asked what was the nature of the 'performance, appli- cant said they did a cakewalk.</p>
        <p>Alderman Wareing : lam sorry to hear you propose to introduce a cakewalk. I consider it rather an indecent exhibition. - -</p>
        <p>The Mayor : Ts it a part of the show, and have they aneared before ? Applicant : Not with the company. They will per- form in pantomime at Liverpool and St. Helens. The Chairman said that, seeing that these children were not called upon direct to take part in connection with the play, they could not see their way to grant the application. Applicant : I think if you refer to the Act I am entitled to it. I have given seven days' notice. The Clerk : The magistrates have discretion, and they have refused the application.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturda§, November 4, 1905.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>WOMAN'S REALM.</head>
        <p>WOMAN'S REALM.</p>
        <p>DR ESS-FASH ,ON-HOME.</p>
        <p>THE AMERICAN GIRL.</p>
        <p>We have heard much of late cf the innreaa- ing stature of the English girl, whose height and breadth have become as much subjects of carica- ture abroad as were at one time the protruding teeth and angularity of the erstwhile typical Britisher. It is interesting to hear that in America the girl of to-day is also assuming gigantic proportions, and bids fair to more tban equal, her English cousins in her increasing dimensions. This is noticeable not only as re- cards the special realm of dressmakers and out- fitters, but in the matter of footgear, stock measurements having had to be altered more than once during the past few years. At one time " sevens " was considered an extra large size in boots or shoes, and one that few women would own up to, whereas nowadays this number is said to be one of those most frequently in de- mand, while " eights " are not considered par- ticularly abnormal, and shoemakers even make a point of keeping a goodly collection of "nines." Gloves are likewise supplied in larger sizes, and although manufacturers have tried to play, up to ,the amour propre of their clients by altering the numbers, so that what is now called "six and a- half " was once "seven," or "seven and a-half," the necessity for little deceptions of this kind is no longer apparent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>USEFUL COAT.</head>
        <p>A USEFUL COAT.</p>
        <p>The costume shewn in the sketch is designed with a view to its being carried out at home, or by the ordinary dressmaker, perfectly plain and tight-fitting coats seldom being quite satittfactory unless tailor-made, and that adds greatly to the expense. The design is suitable for any of the winter materials, one of blue and green tartan being especially smart. The skirt is quite a plain, well-gored shape, and just, clears the ground all round. 'rite material is slightly g:thered in at the yoke, the fulness being (hewn</p>
        <p>tightly down to the waistline, where the long baaque is attached. The yoke and cuffs are of dark-green cloth outlined with a silky black braid. The flat band of green velvet that out- lines the neck takes the place of revers. The ends just meet in front, and are held together wah a hook and eye. The same touch of velvet is repeated on the sleeves, and in the buttons and belt. The waistcoat of white embroidered cloth ia attached to the bolero, and is fastened under tts edge at the left side: or, if preferred, it could be made to wear quite separately.</p>
