      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>Mi:s Lawrence, lady superintendent of the Meath Home for Epileptics. Godalming. has been appointed lady superintendent of the National Hospital for the Paralysed and Epileptic. The new Lady Leigh, better known as Mrs. Dudley Leigh, is one of the handsomest Amen. i can women n society, as well as one of the most popular. She was Miss Frances, Beckwith, a daughter of the Hon. N. M. Beckwith, of New York, and married Lord Leigh in 1890.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>CHILD'S EMPIRE PALETOT.</head>
        <p>CHILD'S EMPIRE PALETOT.</p>
        <p>The paletot pictured herewith is built in the Empire fashion, and for ordinary useful wear it would look well carried out in hunter-green faced cloth. The short waist is outlined with a stitched strap where it joins the "skirts." The little highwayman capes and the, collar are edged with imitation sable, a very good quality of which can be bought quite inexpensively. The loose-fitting sleeves are drawn into turned-,</p>
        <p>back cuffs edged with fur. The fastening of the coat is effected inTisibly. the four large velvet. covered buttons only serving as ornaments. If the coat is intended for best occasions, a cream . .</p>
        <p>r:r,45 ' ' " 1 1 r At: /11 tal 1,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>SCIENCE NOTES AND GLEANINGS.</head>
        <p>SCIENCE NOTES AND GLEANINGS.</p>
        <p>SPINES ON PLANTS</p>
        <p>Some interesting experiments bearing on the subject of the development of spines in plants have been carried on by Dr. L. Cockayne. The plant experimented on is a New Zealand shrub, popularly known as Wild Irishman, which is abundantly furnished with long, sharp spines. The young plants were removed when the spines were beginning to be formed to a moist chamber. %there they were allowed to grow. There they maintained the leafy, spineless habit characteris- tic of seedlings,- shewing that a certain amount of dryness is necessary for the formation of spines.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>GUM-PRODUCING BACILLUS.</head>
        <p>A GUM-PRODUCING BACILLUS.</p>
        <p>The gum which exudes from the fruit of Macrozamia, an Australian plant,' has been found by Mr. - Greig Smith to be of bacterial origin. In the first place, a thin slice of the gum ex- amined under the microscope was found to con- tain a short rod-shaped bacillus. From a por- tion of the stalk of the fruit an organism was isolated by culture. A gum was then prepared from the slime formed by the growth of the microbe, which was found to resemble the natural gum. By the action of wafer, it gave the same sugar-like body. Hence it was con- cluded that the bacillus is the producer of the gum.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>TO ROT-PROOF ROPES.</head>
        <p>TO ROT-PROOF ROPES.</p>
        <p>in order to protect ropes, cords, and cloths made of flax and hemp against rot, the following process is recommended by the Scientific Ameri, can: , The articles should bo left for four days in a solution of copper sulphate-20 grammes tc the litp—after which they should be dried, and arm, in order to prevent the copper sulphate being washed out by water, they should be placed in a solution of soap-100 grammes to the gallon—an insoluble copper soap being formed. In order to secure the same result with twine, it must be placed for an hour in a solutgon of glue, after which it must be dried, and then soaked in a solution of tannin. When taken from the tannin, it is to be again dried and then soaked in oil. The treatment wits glue and tannin, how- ever, is apt to make the twine too stiff, and se impair its usefulness, and the first-mentioned treatment can be adopted if desired. STRETCHES OF IMAGINATION.</p>
        <p>How large is the sea? This is a bold hig question, and any possible answer involves a considerable stretch of the imagination. Here, says a contemporary, is a startling illustration of its vast volume:</p>
        <p>If the water of the sea could be gathered into a round column, reaching the 93,000,000 miles which separate us from the sun, the diameter of this column would be nearly two miles and a.half !</p>
        <p>It is perhaps even more difficult to realise that this mighty mass of waters could be dissi- pated in, a few moments, if the column we have imagined could become ice, and if the entire heat of the sun could be concentrated upon it. All would be melted in one second 01 time, and converted into steam in eight seconds!</p>
        <p>ANIMAL ELECTRICITY.</p>
        <p>Some electrical phenomena in the animal kingdom were simultaneously receiving atten- tion, says a writer in "Electricity in the Service of Man." Iteaumur. iabout 1714) pointed out that the. electrical shad-fish was capable of im- parting violent shocks. These he attributed to the muscular power of the animal's tail. It was afterwards assumed that these shocks might be of an electrical nature, and this was proved ex- perimentally by Dr. John Walsh in 1772. He experimented on the electrical shad-fish, and showed that in order to obtain the shock the fish must be touched on both sides at the same time. Many experiments in different directions were made to solve the problem as to the source of the electricity of the torpedo and electric eel, but oven up to the present time much un- certainty prevails.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>NITROGEN-FIXING BACTERIA.</head>
        <p>NITROGEN-FIXING BACTERIA.</p>
        <p>It has been known now for some time that many of the higher plants depend to a large extent on the lower for their supplies of nitro- gen. On the roots of the kidney bean and other leguminous plants • numerous little tuber-like growths may be seen. These are caused by nitrogen-fixing bacteria, and the fixed nitrogen is afterwards used by the plant. Mr. G. T. Moore, of tht+ United States Department of Agriculture, makes some important additions to our knowledge of the subject. He finds that, if grown in nitrogenous media, these bacteria lose their power of fixing nitrogen, and also of infect- ing leguminous plants; while in non-nitro- genous media they retain both these properties. This probably explains the previous failure of the attempts to supply cultures of the, organisms to be used by growers. Mr. Moore, however, has devised a method by which cultures of one of these nitrogen-fixing bacteria can be put up and distributed. They are grown in nitrogen-free media, and dried on cotton wool immersed in the culture. These are sent out along with packages of nutrient salts. The grower who wishes to use them must first make a culture with the materials provided, ano then use this to inocu- late the soil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>WONDERS OF A SOLAR ECLIPSE.</head>
        <p>WONDERS OF A SOLAR ECLIPSE.</p>
        <p>Professor Henry E. Armstrong, writing in School on the eclipse of the sun, says that of the event itself it is difficult to give a description the glory of it is indescribable. "Let astro- nomers, if they will, in future speak of eclipses; but let us poor men in the street think of an effulgence of divine glory as coming into view when the main body of the sun is blotted out- by the moon. Up to the moment of totality nothing is seen; the eye is blinded by the sun's brilliance; then, on the instant, an outer sun is born with magic haste; without click or break to announce its appearance, the corona shines forth around the velvet black disc of the dead sun, a wondrous new light extending far out into space. The silent suddenness of its entry upon the scene is in itself marvellous. Its dis- appearance is equally sudden—it is instan- taneously killed by the emerging sunbeams; andi perhaps the most striking aspect of the pheno7 menon is the impression which is produced at this stage of the marvellous illuminating power of, so to speak, the least little bit of real sun."</p>
        <p>• A RIIGE CATHERINE WHEEL. Professor Armstrong gives the following ex- ample to describe the eclipse: " Those who have played with fireworks in their youth know well the appearance of a catherine wheel near to its end—the emergence of the black disc within the irregular whirling circle of fire, at the same time that this is crossed by lateral streamers due to tho piercing of the case, so that fire no longer issues only from the central tube. Such, more or less, was the aspect of the totally eclipsed sun—that of a huge, black-centred, silver fire catherine wheel near to its end, but betraying no indication whatever of motion; on the(' contrary, one of awe-inspiring stillness and indescribable loveli- ness. Its illuminating effect on the present occasion was surprisingly great—most of us, in fact, had expected to see the corona against a far darker background. Near to the black disc the light was very bright, but it diminished rapidly in intensity outwards, from silver white to an ethereal blue haze."</p>
        <p>-, blanket cloth is becoming and smart, the fur edging harmonising so well with the deep cream- THE PERMANENCE OF OCEAN BASINS. colour. In this case brown velvet buttons should A collection of rocks brought to Capetown be used, and, and a hat of soft brown beaver, trimmed with rosettes of glossy cream wash- from Tristan d'Acunha leads Mr. Schwarz, of the Geological Survey of Cape Colony, to discuss Mg silk, various controversial subjects connected with the QUERN VICTORIA'S DAIRY EXPERT. physics of the earth's crust. In the collection Miss May Griffiths, who has been appointed to there is a novelty of some interest in the form of a block of gntsiss picked up in the neighbotirhood superintend the new model dairy built by Sir -- 'of the settlement at Edinburgh. That this rock Ernest Cassel at his country house near New. 1 market, is a daughter of the late Mr. J. was not brought to the island by some external agency soems confirmed, in Mr. Schwarz's view, Griffiths, of Penally Court, Pembrokeshire. For by the occurrence, among rocks from Nightin- four years Miss Griffiths superintended the late gale Island, of fragments of porphyritic rock em- Queen's dairy at Balmoral. Her new charge is one of the finest dairies in the country. bedded in the lavas. They are of minute size, and have evidently been derived from the shat- THE REVIVAL OF CORAL. tering of a foreign igneous mass of an acid type Old coral necklaces are worn now by blondes, by explosions, sbch, as occur E( and the demand for them is increasing their IN TH THROATS OF VOLCANOES, value. There is a great deal to learn about and thus cannot have been drifted to the island. soral. The best kinds are sold by the grain While allowing that the evidence is not as good ' as one could wish for. Mr. Schwarz thinks it Pale pink is to be had as small as a pea, or as • reasonable to suppose that this granite belongs large as a florin. Some of the big old brooches, veritable breastplates, which our grandmothers to t