        <p>ST. PETER'S SALE OF WORK. RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL. The annual sale of work in connection with St. Peter's Church was opened on Monday in the school- room, John-street. The VICAR, who presided over a large attendance at the opening ceremony, at the outset heartily thanked all who had worked in connection with the sale. One of the good points of such an event as that was that it served to bring the workers together and enabled people to contribute small sums who otherwise would not be able to do their share in helping forward the work of the parish. Their liabilities were not less than they were last year. Tn 1901 they had spent on the Curate's fund and the Mission fund the sum of £2ll. Last year £79 of that amount was obtained from ths sale of work which also contributed £2O to other objects. He hoped they would do equally as well that year. (Applause.) The MAYOR, AYR° was heartily received, said he was pleased to be of service especially as Mr. Percival was a particular friend of his. He was also pleased to be able to do something to further the good objects of the sale, which he had pleasure in declaring open. (Applause.) Councillor John ROBERTS proposed a vote of thanks to the Mayor for his services and to the Mayoress for her presence. In their parish they were fortunate in having a large band of capable workers, and he was sure that if the work was carried on in the future as it had been in the past t. Peter's was bound to progress (Applause.) Mr. T. S. STEEL seconded and the vote was heartily accorded. The MAYOR briefly replied. The stalls, which contained ample evidences of the enthusiasm and industry of the promoters of the sale were in charge of the following ladies : Vicarage stall Mesdames Percival,John Taylor, Joseph Tay!or,lieacock, Cowdell and J. Wilkinson and the Misses Young ; re- freshment stall: Mesdames Bamforth. Lingley, Webster. F. Webster,and Yenmans,Miss Stanley and Miss Barlow; flower stall:Mrs. Clayton and Miss Boardman;toy stall : Misses Lily and Dolly Percival and Miss Taylor ; sweet stall : Miss Hughes, and Miss Percival ; bran-tub, the Misses Wilkinson.</p>
        <p>At the re-opening of the sale on Tuesday afternoon the Vicar announced with regret that Mrs. Frost, who had promised to perform the opening ceremony, was unable to be present owing to the death of her uncle, Mr. Peter Stubs. He was sure all present greatly regretted the cause of her absence. Continuing the Vicar said the proceeds for the previous day amounted to .£SG ss. Bd, and he hoped the result of Tuesday's pro- ceedings would realize anticipations. Having co.a- mended the objects of the sale. Mr. Percival formally declared it open. The amusement during the sale included various com- petitions which were entered into with zest.</p>
        <p>Arnold Alexander, when charged at Cardiff on ruesday with attempting to murder his mother, said he was sorry he did not finish her. He would do so when he came out.</p>
        <p>INGTON.</p>
      </div>
