      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>THEY WILL MAKE</head>
        <p>THEY WILL MAKE</p>
        <p>Outbreak of Chicken-pox at Stockton</p>
        <p>Heath.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Rum' Dis- trict Council was held at Dutton on Tuesday when there were present :—Messrs. S. Woodward (presiding), C. Roylance, J. Bolshaw, H. Eyes, P. Mountleld, U. Egerton Warburton, J. Stokes, J. Whitlow, A. Broome, T. Baxter. H. M. Davies, J. Smith, T. Wright, W. Harrison, G. Pilling, S. Horton, J. L. Jones, E. Bretherton, R. A. Chrimes, J. Fryer, J. Spruce, J. Whitlow, T. Antwis, Admiral Jones-Parry, and Messrs. G. F. Ashton (clerk), W. E. Hough (assistant clerk), W. Diggle and J. S.vinton (surveyors), J. Farrington (inspector), and Dr. Adams (medical offinr).</p>
        <p>THE SEE/RE OF BAD FOOD. The clerk reported the receipt of a communication from the Manchester Port Sanitary Authority stating that the authority had no power to seize and destroy unsound articles intended for the food of man. From the large consignments of articles brought into the port, they were of the opinion that it would be a dis- tinct advantage if they had those powers. Food unflt for consumption could he more easily detected as it was unloaded from vessels. They intended to apply to the Local Government Board for the powers and asked for a resolution supporting the application. It was decided to support the application. PROPOSED NEW ROAD.</p>
        <p>Mr. Lake, law clerk of the Runcorn Urban Council, wrote :--[ am instructed by my Highways Committee to ask whether the Parish Council of Weston are pro- pared to consider the old proposal to construct a road from the Runcorn Railway Station. I find that the representatives of the late Major Orred are prepared to give the question much more favourable consideration than when the matter was before us last and that the scheme is consequently much more feasible. A committee consisting of Messrs. S. Woodward, G. Pilling, T. Baer, J. Stoke and the two Weston repre- sentatives was appointed to consider the question.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>NORLEY WATEIt SUPPLY.</head>
        <p>NORLEY WATEIt SUPPLY.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. F. Bell,chairman of the Norley Parish Council I wrote that at a meeting held on the 13th October the 1 Council resolved : " That this Council unanimously I feel that the time is not yet ripe for a water scheme of any kind and that they can offer the Rural District Council of Runcorn no support of any kind in the matter of the water supply. ' Mr. Bell attended the meeting of the council to state,l his views. Asked by the chairmb.n how he proposed I that the question should be dealt with he said that so I far he knew there was no way at all of dealing with it. No one in the parish wanted a water scheme all being satisfied with the present supply. The water supply I had never resulted in an outbreak of disease.</p>
        <p>The Chairman stated that the District -e-o.uncil were satisfied that the water supply at Norley was not what it ought to be, and as the public health authority they had to see that it was remedied. They were moved only by the requirements of the district. After a long discussion it was decided to ask the landowners in the parish of Norley to repair the exist- ing wells and to endeavour to purify the water and to inform the Local Government Board of the course pro- posed, pointinr, out to the Board that the financial position of the parish would not enable them to meet the expense of a scheme so extensive as the one suggested. THE HEALTH OF THE DISTRICT. Dr. Adams reported that three cases of scarlet fever lux' occurred in Stockton Heath. In Stockton Heath I there had also been an outbreak of chicken-pox and I mea:ales.' He had ordered the infant schools to be closed for a fortnight. It might be necessary also to close the other schools.</p>
        <p>It was stated on Tuesday by Mr. John Williams, heed of a well-known firm of provision dealers in Manchester, that one firm was making .£2,000 per week out of butter-faking. It has been decided that an impression of the date stamp shall be made on the counterfoil of a postal order at the time of issue of the order, when the pur- chaser asks that this shall be done. '</p>
      </div>
