      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>ANOTHER IN WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>ANOTHER IN WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>I Another interesting Worringten case is given here to- day. A story of good, safe help received, is proved after it has stoad a test of years. Warrington seeks no better proof. Mr. Thomas Ruane, who lives at 16, King street, War- rington, says :—" For years I suffered from severe back- aches, and urinary troubles. There were also symptoms of gravel. Tne pains in my back were at times so acute that I was obliged to leave my work, and go borne. A cabman as you know, has to be out in all weathers, and my hours for taking meals are very irregular. I have no doubt that aggravated my trouble to a great extent. I also suffered with bad headaches,indigestion, and poor appetite ; in fact, I was generally run down. When I heard of Doan's Backache Kidney Pills, I thought I would try them, because they gave local proof that they were able to cure kidney cure. I got some, and the relief they gave me was marvellous. In a very short time they drove the pains right away, and I felt greatly improved in my general health. I give you permission to publish this, so that alters may hear of this great kidney specific, which I believe is capable of curing the worst case of kidney complaint." One of our representatives was in Warrington two and half years later, and had the pleasure of calling on Mr. Ruane. In the course of a most interesting chat, he enquired " Speaking about your testimonial which has been appearing in the papers lately, Mr. Rurine,how has your health been since your cure ? You look well enough, I must say." " And I feel well enough," Mr. Ruane replied : " I never felt, better in 311 my life. The good the pills did me was not only thorough, but has been able to stand the test of timt. Although it was over two and a half years ago now that I was cured, I have been in the best of health ever since. I luxe never come across such a splendid medicine in my life as Doan's Pills. My wife and I always takes real pleasure in recommending them to anyone whom we know to be suffering from kidney disorders. Doan's backache kidney pills are two shillings and ninepence per box (six boxes for thirteen shillings and ninepence.) Of all chemists and stores, or post free, on receipt of price, direct, from Foster- McClellan Co., 8, Wells-street,Oxford-street, Lonclon,W. Be sure you get the same kind of pills as Mr. Roane ad.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held on Wednesday, Mr. .1. Billinge presiding. There were also present :—Mrs. Clay, Mrs. Wilkinson, Miss Broadbent, Messrs. W. Hickman, A. J. Jolley, B. Forshaw, S. Davies, M. W. Seton, G. Mather, A. Brooks, J. C. Sutton (clerk), J. F. Awbery (master) and A. Wright (superintendent of radgate Schools).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>DEATH OF AN OI,D- GUARDIAN:</head>
        <p>DEATH OF AN OI,D- GUARDIAN:</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN referred to the loss the Board had sustained by the death of Mr. John Turton who repro' seated the East Ward of the Urban Distric- of linydock. It had been his (the chairman's) privilege to know Mr. Turton from infancy and as one got older in life he learned to appreciate fully the valuable services their late colleague rendered. It might well be said of a man a his type that he was a „self made man." He was born nearly 71 years ago in Ashton of humble parents and came to Ilaydock as a boy. He commenced work in the mine at eight years of age and was so assiduous in his attention to duty that he gradually obtained promotion until he became manager of two important collieries. For a quarter of a century he figured in public life. He served efficiently on the old local Board and afterwards on the Urban District Council. He was one of the managers of the Cottage Hospital and took a deep interest in charities, being the founder of the old folk's treat. The chairman concluded by moving a vote of condolence with the widow and family. Mr. S. DAVIES seconded. It was his privilege to work with Mr. Turton in his better days and he always found him to be a man i.vhoso advice and opinion were highly valued. He was a thorough, straightforward, conscien- tious man. Mr. Davies mentioned that the funeral of Mr. Turton, which he attended, was the fifteenth funeral of a member of the Board at which he had been present. Mr. HICKMAN, Mr. FORSHAW, MT. BROOKS and the CLERK supported the motion which was carried, all the ' members standing. 1 The CLERK was instructed to formally declare the vacancy in East Ward, Haydock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>LECTURES TO PROBATIONERS.</head>
        <p>LECTURES TO PROBATIONERS.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Hot;O. Fo;lti;i, ' it was resolved that the offer of Dr. Braicle to give a weekly couise of lectures to the probationer Nurses be accepted, and that the amount of the honorarium for these additional services be fixed at the completion of the course. This recommendation was approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, November 4, 1905.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, November 4, 1905.</p>
        <p>01? LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>St. John's Presbyterian Church.</p>
        <p>Special services were held in the above church on Sunday in connection with the" John Knox Quarter- Centenary Celebrations." At the evening service the Rev. J. L. Milne took for his text : What can man do unto me ? " He gave a sketch of the life of John Knox. who, he said, was practically the founder of the Scottish Presbyterian Church. He was a man who was never afraid to face a man, but he wts greatly in advance of his time, if one could say that. As a politician, he demaaded freedom of conscience. As an educationalist he believed that the educttion of the children was the duty of the secular power, and it ought to be compul- sory and free. During the service special anthems were rendered by the choir, Mr. Jowett presiding at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>An open meeting of this Society was held on Sunday last, when there was a good gathering. When Mr. .1. I W. Slack presided and spoke upon" Getting and Grow- ing." He referred to the getting of Whittaker Wright, which proved to him a great loss. He was a man who built a wall 10 feet high and 12 miles round, enclosing a fairy Palace, and beautiful lake. He gained a little world and lost his Soul of life and purity. AR our wealth, and talents should be accounted for to God. If we could not be rich we could be good. The speaker, , M-. J. Walley, of Manchester, spoke upon " The Rock of I Ages "its arm foundation, and the beautiful Structures that can be built upon it. It was not his fortune to be rich, but rather than gain riches le, unholy means, he would rather die poor and be buried in a pauper's grave , The Apollo Quartette party sang " Rock of Ages "and I " Remember Me " in a pleasing manner. Revs. G. Slack ! and John Yonge also took part in the meeting, Mr. Payton Mos. Bac. presided at the organ. Mr. Yonge welcomed two new members. A good collection was I taken. Special hymns prepared by the Lancashire !Federation were very heartily sung.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
