        <p>Amp," said Frazer' in In_ iriendliest fishios, " as I wanted to have a good chat to our- selves, so as to get to know each other better. In club-life one has so many acquaintances, but so few friends." Ben was secretly annoyed, but felt bound to murmur that "it was awfully nice of him " ; and then they made their way to the big dining-room; where many of the most Important international questions of their time had often been discussed on neutral ground over a bottle of wine " without I prejudice." Frazer's first move was to order a diplo- matic bottle of bubbling wine, and then he laid himself out to make himself as charming is he could to his guest, and to efface the bad Lmpression he knew the other had of him. Ben was no churl, and was quite ready to meet his host half-way, as he began to warm to the wine and his conversation. Frazer ould talk extremely well when he cared to, or when it was worth his while, and his guest by the end of the dinner had begun to ask him- self seriously whether he ought not, to re- consider or, at any rate, modify his original dislike. " By the way," said Frazer, cracking a nut, when they had reached the port stage, " I aave an invitation for you to-night. if you would care to accept it." " It entirely depends on whom it is from," answered the other, laughing. "I am not a • great society man." " I don't know about that," r?niiaed his aost, smiling shrewdly. " There are rumours lbout you ; but rumour is a lying jade. The • invitation I mentioned is from the Duchess of Hanipstead." • The Duchess of Hampstead ? " echoed Ben in surprise. " When was it given, may I ask ? "</p>
        <p>" This very afternoon that ever was, soon ! aftcr I met you in the club," answered lan Frazer. " I was callir:g on her you know, I'm secretary of her pet committee, which is her latest hobby and she reminded me that she was giving a reception to-night to the members. I told her that von were dining with mc, and she suggested that I should bring you, as you were a most suitable person to. join." "I? " said Ben, in surprise. " And what is the raison detre of this committee ?" That, my dear fellow," replied his host, cracking another nut, " you will learn when won arrive. At orescnt it is a</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>THE. ..</head>
        <p>THE. ..</p>
        <p>WHITE ROSE.</p>
        <p>By GERALD BISS.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XXL—Continued)</p>
        <p>On this occasion the ceremony of drawing did not last long. As youngest, Branksea was the first to step forward and draw a ticket.</p>
        <p>Then with a strange smile, half of triumph, he showed it to the company. On it was the black cross.</p>
        <p>Lady Swanage bit her lip to, suppress an exclamation, and her husband first grew more fiery red and then ashy pale. It was a chance he had hardly considered. A murmur went round the table.</p>
        <p>"Short and sharp, your Majesty," said Branksca in his rough voice, as he bowed to Otto. "You may rely upon me to. do what. ever is for the best without fear or favour."</p>
        <p>Otto only bowed in reply without a word, and a dead silence fell on the room.</p>
        <p>To all it was something of a personal relief, but the actual horror of shedding blood had itself diminished as .success after success had whetted their appetites. Portcullis alone eyed Branksea strangely. He was wondering whether it would affect him in the same way and make him see things in dark corners when they were not there. Then a shiver ran down his back.</p>
        <p>• A moment later the silence was broken, and the committee began to discuss reports, docu- ments and plans in detail, until a knock came at the door.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>CHAPTER XXII.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XXII.</p>
        <p>MEANWHILE, despite Bina's letter, Ben had spent a lonely and intermittently miserable day. Personally he was the least mercenary of men, and the comparatively small income he possessed had always been ample for his own needs, and would, to his mind, even admit of marriage on a very modest scale. _Moreover,</p>
        <p>"As the old yokel said, my dear fellow," rejoined Frrtzer. laughing, "` It pleases they and it don't hurt we.' Besides it furthet pleases her Grace to take this new phase . unusually seriously, as you will see ; and she is determined in dik, course to spring a mine an the public without exciting the least premonitory suspicion." "But what is the main idea?" pursued Ben obstinately. "Surely .you can give me an inkling before we arrive ? " "I should get myself into serious disfavour at headouarters if I dicl," laughed Frazer. " They lif:c to have their explanation?to make themselves. It's political, not charitable ; I can say as much as that." "A. sort of new Primrose League, I sup- pose ? " sugcrested Ben more lightly. " You will know before long,' said the driver abruptly. "Here is Hampstead Rouse." The car swerved off the main road and ran up to the great gates. ' I'don't see any lights," said Ben, looking round. " I wonder if we are too late ? " "Part of the mystery," answered Frazer ; and he proceeded to give the signal, whistling the old Jacobite refrain softly but clearly.</p>
        <p>The gate swung open noiselessly, and the car shot through into the front garden. " Hallo ! " exclaimed Ben, who was more mystified than ever, " there are quite a lot &gt;f motors waiting."</p>
        <p>"Its the only-possible way of reaching an naccessible place like this at night with ;ecreCy," explained his cicerone. "We get 3nt here." ,</p>
        <p>Both sprung down from the car, and Frazer led the way to the front door. Here again be cave the signal by a low whistle and it opened instantly on the chain.</p>
        <p>" Who goes there ?" asked the thin voice of tri old woman. " Bridge." responded Frazer clearly. The chain was shot back softly in the we;l-oiled socket, and they were admitted without further delay. Ben had to acknowledge to himself that he was beginning to feel decidedly uncomfortable, but it was obviously too late to draw back. It flashed through his mind that the mysthry might perhaps be more serious that he had at first suspected; and the only consolation he .found in his thoughts was the remembrance that Bina was not involved in it. If, after all, it should prove not to be serious, what then was the use of all this absurd tom- foolery. He was aroused by Frazer's voice. "Leave your coat here," he said, !" and then follow me."</p>
        <p>Obeying instructions, Ben divested him- self of his heavy coat and followed Frazer • through the dark hall. They stopped in front of a door, and his :ompanion knocked.</p>
        <p>Ben looked as puzzled as he felt. He, per. the honour of inviting you tojoin sonallY, did not care' a fig for the committee Ben was beginning to recover froth the and its attendant mystery, but, at the same -first shock, though a and one [ ime, he old not wtsh to oliena tne oneness. thoughts and suspicions were passing through I Was it possibly an olive branch he wondered 7 his brain.„ And the object of ' your White Rose "She spoke very cordially of you," eon- Lea gee ? " he asked, wondering whether the tinued Frazer, eyeing his guest closely and ... playing him delicately, " and said that if you emblem could be a coincidence or whether he hesitated I was to press you. She hinted at had got at the root of London's sensational some slight disagreement without specifying mr The object is one with which you of all te. any particular cause; but gave me to under- stand that that was all fixed up now." I people, considering your name and family A glow of satisfaction spread through Ben's -history," answered the duke, "should be heart and over his face, and he surrendered entirely in sympathy: That is why we have pleasurably to Frazer, retracting all harsh .aitsk,,,cl__y_aee. you .. here to-piglit..t4r,t.a;lte the oath of judgments. He felt the clouds roll away 'u ,ee or ..e. earn e_ _ i ..... for Bina and himself, and he was 'overjoyed. As he grew more decided as to the serious- "Of course, I shall be delighted, my dear ness of the meeting, Ben grew more cool and chap," he said warmly. "The duchess's will, collected, and he began to wonder vaguely is law in all matters." whether his life might be at stake, absurd as " That's good," said Frazer, concealing it seemed. his diplomatic triumph, as many another had "But your Grace has not stated what that been concealed at the Cosmopolitan. " But object is," interposed Branksea, as though . you won't see Lady Bina there," he added eager for information. "You must remem- with a roguish twinkle, "as she is not yet ber th.l, too, was awaiting the arrival of of the duchess's elect." Lord-Ben Nevis for initiation." . "That I know," admitted Ben, frankly' . had advanced towards the table and stood He rose and took his stand beside Ben, who accepting Fraser's friendly dig in good part n ' and not resenting the familiarity in his de- facing the silent gathering. light at the change in affairs; "but let us There was a momentary pause, and then hoop that she, too, will join."' the duke spoke again in a measured voice. "The object, gentlemen, is to reinstate the Frazer proposed an adjournment to the smoking-room, and the pair grew quite House of Stuart upon'. the throne of this confidential over two long cigars till the time country, to bring theta back after centuries of arrived to start, exile, and to right the wrong done to them I'll run you up in my little car," said and to many others who fought anicl. bled and " lost everything in their sacred cause —of Frazer. " We've got to go all the way to Hampstead House." whom," he added, "not the least was the h "Oh," exclaimed Ben in surprise, and then house of Ben Nevis, which you represent." Ben's bewilderment was obvious. He was ae added : "But that 'will interest me as I lave never seen it." wondering whether the affair could be serious, "It's a grand old place," said the other, conscious only that it was Bina's father who snthnsiasticallY ; "but it's a cold drive, and was speaking. To him the idea was soabsurd, so preposterous, that it amounted to you'll need a thicker coat. Run round to little short of mania. Yet, if sincere, it was a your rooms and fetch one, while I scribble a very grave matter—terribly grave if it actual- note I must write and go round to the garage ly involved the responsdiility of the White for my car. I'll pick you up at your chem.- bars. I shall he there as soon as you are." Rose murders. "That's splendid," said Ben ; and he "Sir," he said to the duke, " frankly -I am parted from Frazer in the hall in front of two bewildered, and I am at a loss to understand N three- members and some of the club how to take this matter. Is it an organised " 3ervants, none of whom, however, had heard piece of buffoonery for my benefit? a The duke flushed angrily, and one or twois lest words, which had been spoken in a 3tudiedlv low voice. . of the others started up. Carruthers alone smiled. At the door Bcn Nevis half turned and e Your lordship is pleased to jest," replied "Good-night, 01l chap," Frazer called out d i waved his hand. . -. the older man hotly. " You o not know n whose presence you stand. Yonder in that to him, as the doors swung to, in a voice that chair," pointing to Otto, "sits King Charles was heard and afterwards remembered. I. our royal master, who for the first time In a few minutes they were seated side by to-night has honoured our deliberations." side in 'an electric runabout, flying through Ben looked across the room at Otto, who the crisp night air to Hampstead: sat drumming the fingers of his right hand on " I can't understand the object of all this the table. Their eves met, and each regret- mystery," said Ben, as they approached the fully felt that under different auspices they Heath. ." It seems so out of place in the might have been friends. This then explain- twentieth eenturvi. ..i ed the este af " Mr. St, -1," id the</p>
        <p>te mystery ol .uart, an deference. shown to him. Then it flashed across him that it was he whom they wanted Bina to marry, so that she might be a Queen ! This was the object of the ambitious duke and duchess, and he saw it now. What madness it all was, and .to what wicked lengths it would lead, or had led, if the committee were indeed responsible for the horrors which bore the brand of their emblem! Ben laughed a harsh, nervous, unnatural laugh. ' If serious. your Grace," he said calmly and coolly, holding himself well in hand by a strong effort, " what madness, what folly, for you, the first peer of the realm, to sacrifice all—(p " For God and the right I " interrupted the duke, quoting his tag fanatically. "For an impossible, undesirable, and un- necessary change, involving crime and bloodshed—not," he added almost wistfully, "that I would connect the honbured names here with the recent shocking series of crimes." He paused interrogatively. All were silent, some half abashed at his strange candour. Again it fell to the Duke of Hampstead by right of his position to act as spokesman. "We stand or fall to a greater master than you, Lord Ben Nevis." "I can't believe it, oh, my God, I can't believe it ! " broke out Ben, losing control of his demeanour, as he thought of Bina. "If anyone of the House of Stuart wishes to test my loyalty to their cause in days gone by, let him look at what is written in the pages of history, As to the present, I am no fatuous dreamer of idle dreams, no conspirator against rny country's good, for the sake of dead and gone ideals ; and, moreover, I am proud to bow my knee to the great dynasty which has helped to make England great, loyal to the King in whose service I have fought and shed blood. If you, one and all, and you, Mr. Stuart—" he was interrupted for an instant by a clamour of protest round the table, but he went on without heeding it—" wish other- wise, you are mad, mad, MAO; and God forgive you all I" • Angry cries resounded from all parts of the room. Otto rose with his hand raised, and silence fell on his supporters. "You are a brave man, Lord Ben Nevis," he said very quietly. " We should be honoured if you will join us." He sat down again. "Sir," answered Ben, gazing at him sadly, "my fealty is sworn to another, and I am a soldier." Otto waved his hand. "Let him go," he said, and covered his eyes for an instant. It was terrible to think that he was going to his death. Then Branksea spoke, repeating his lesson pat like a school-child, without expression or emotion. "Ben Nevis, I am with you. I will be no traitor to my King. Gentlemen, I cannot join you." A feigned murmur went round the room. "But your father and your mother ? asked Ben in a low voice. ' "I cannot help that," answered the other. "They should have warned me." They turned to leave the room together. Ellis Gordon was at the door with the key in 'his hand.</p>
        <p>"Lord Ben Nevis, Lord 13ranksea," said the duke, speaking anxiously, " you will take the oath of silence ? You will swear not to reveal what you have seen and heard to- night ? " "I cannot take any oath till I have thought," answqed Ben sadly. " I have not only mysclf to Think of, but my conscience and my duty; and God help me to do what is right." "I, too, must think," said Branksea; "but remember, in your midst I have strong ties." And he turned away, following Ben into the dark hall. "Let me drive you into town, Ben Nevis," he said in a low voice; " I have my car here." "Thank you," said the other shortly. His mind and heart were too full for talking. Branksea stepped into the driver's seat and as Ben got in beside him, started the car. But one thing, as the car sprang forward, struck Ben in the pale cold moonlight. It was a white rose in the driver's buttonhole, and in an instant the whole dastardly plot flashed through his mind. He was driving beside his predestined murderer In some way he must stop the car and get away. Now he could understand the whole by-play, and saw that his life was but a counter to these conspirators in their scheme. Of Branksea he had little fear in a fair fight ; but his opponent was armed, and he was, not. Taken unawares, he could easily have dis- posed of him before he had time to realise what was happening; but now he was warned. .</p>
        <p>Branksea was watching , him with the gleaming eyes of a savage beast, waiting his opportunity-. He had decided to follow Portcullis's movements in exact detail, as they appeared to him the safest and surest. His only miscalculation was that he did not dream that a moonbeam and a white flower had betrayed him to his victim; so he anticipated no resistance. Near the fatal clump of trees the car, aided by its owner, began. to show signs of going wrong. Suddenly it stopped dead. "Damn," exclaimed Branksea, speaking for the first time, "there's something wrong. I believe the gear has jammed." 4</p>
