      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>CHAPTER XXIII.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XXIII.</p>
        <p>There was a pause, followed by the sound of the key being put into the lock. ." Who goes there ? " asked Gordon's voice. " Bridge," again answered lan Frazer. •</p>
        <p>The key grated in t.he lock, and the heavy oak door was thrown open, revealing the brivhtly-lighted room within..</p>
        <p>The sudden stream of light dazzled Ben Nevis as he followed Frazer, and for a second lie seemed unable to see anything. Then his bewildered eyes began to take in the scene, little by little. The old baronial hall with its roof and pannelling of black oak: the bright clusters of white light: the big open fire-place glowing red ; the dais with its long table covered with gold plate the remains of dessert, and howls of white flowers ; and, right in front of nim, the other table running clown the centre Df the dining hall, round which men and women were seated, obviously serious. . . .</p>
        <p>At the head of the table, with a start he recognised "Mr. Stuart," and near him the Duchess of Hampstead, the Marchiones of Swanage, and others he had met. Fusther down his eye caught sight of Atherley, Port- cullis, Carruthers, and then nearer, Lord struan and Lord Stepney, and at the end of the table by the door the Duke of Hampstead, ext to him, with his back to the door, he recognised the black head oi Branksea. These impressions flashed upon his be- wildered brain, one after the other, and he had not properly recovered himself when the duke rose and advanced, bolding out his hand.</p>
        <p>"Welcome. Lord Ben Nevis," he said with a forced cordiality, "to our deliberations. I trust that before the evening is much older that we shall have the pleasure of enrolling you as a member of our committee." Ben took the proffered hand and was about to answer, when his eyes fell on the white rose in the duke's button-hole. For a moment he stood tongue-tied, fascinated ; and then he glanced slowly round the table, noting in each coat a similar emblem. For a second his nerve seemed to give under the strain.</p>
        <p>" White—white roses I " he exclaimed, hoarsely. The idea seemed to fascinate him to the, exclusion of everything else. Several of those at the table started up, but the duke was splendid in his sang froid. "We are the Head Centre of the White Rose League, Lord Ben Nevis," he replied in a hard metallic voice to the newcomer's un- spoken qncstion. " which we are doing you</p>
      </div>
