      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>NOVEMBER 4th 1805.</p>
        <p>THE terms demanded THE UNEMPLOYED, by the present leaders of the unemployed in London will be generally pronounced to be pre- posterous. They are, in brief, that the Govern- ment or the local authorities shall provide full work at full wages. There seems to be an idea that the Government is a gold mine, and that the municipal bodies are able to draw on unlimited funds. The plain matter of .fact is that the Govern- ment has nothing which it does not take from the taxpayer, and that the local authorities have noth- ing which they do not receive from the Govern- ment, or which they do not levy in the form of rates. Suppose the demand of the leaders of the unemployed were to be complied with, what would happen ? If the men at present out of employment were put to necessary work, their competition would throw out an equal,or almost equal,nunaber of workmen so efficient that they have been able to keep their places in bad times. If the work were not necessary—if it were a mere finding of work, in fact—the wages would have to be paid by the community at large, by the industrious poor as well as by the leisured wealthy. The man • who was in useful employment would be labouring not only for himself and his family, but for a man engaged in employment which, at the best, might become useful some years hence. This would be the'case, of course, whether the relief work pro- vided was paid for at the full rate of wages or on some lower scale ; but whilst the pro- vision of temporary employment would, under certain conditions, be an act of benevolence it would, under the conditions demanded, be a piece of reckless and ruinous national extra- vagance. We do not deny that there is some logic in the demand. If it be granted that it is the duty of the State to find work for the un- employed, it must either find them quite useless and unprofitable work, in which case it could make its own terms, or it must, find them work which would come into competition with the work done under private employers, and should, therefore, be paid for at the usual rate of wages. "To work for leas Oen the established rate," says one of the leaders of the unemployed, " would be to become blacklegs." What is asked for, in fact, is not relief in times of distress, but regular work at established rates of wages. It is scarcely necessary to add that the unemployed move- ment has been captured by the Socialists. This is plainly declared by LI nature of the demands that are made. The ultimate idea is to make the State the universal employer of labour, as it would soon inevitably become if the principle were conceded that it is bound to find work for every unemployed man, and to pay him the wages that he would receive from a private employer. It will be impossible to deal with the unemployed problem if the parties on one side approach it in this impracticable spirit. It is a problem by 110 means simple. It is, on the contrary, surrounded by all manner of difficulties. It is the deepest and the oldest of all the social problems. The fact that it is, not solved is proof that the right solution has evaded all the wisest minds of the world in all ages. The Socialists appear to think that everything is to be put right by a formula. But that is not so. If the unemployed question is to be dealt with at all wisely and beneficially we must get back to right lines. Any work provided for the unemployed 'must not be of a character which would make work under private employers undesirable, nor must it be paid for at a rate which would destroy the motive for seeking work elsewhere. These prin- ciples, at least, may be postulated. Relief work should be as pronouncedly different RS possible from regular employment. The practical applies tion of the ideas of the leaders of the London unemployed would simply lead to national disaster.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>p</p>
        <p>The Town Council "THE STATEMENT OP TUE emerged from its CASE." treatment on Taw day of Councillor Lord's motions re the statement of the case again two members in a very bad light. In fact the proceedings eventually developed into a fare° tinged with occasional outbursts of passion such as have become all too frequent in recent times' The agenda contained resolutions by Councillor Lord calling upon the Town Clerk to lay the state- ment of the case before the Investigation Com- mittee, prior to sending it to the Attorney-General- This was almost the last business of the meeting ; the minutes of all committees had been read, discussed, and confirmed. In the ordinary cool's° of procedure Councillor Lord spoke to and moved his resolutions in spite of a hint that they were out of order. Eventually Councillor Clegg raised a point of order, and the Mayor ruled that the motions were not admissible, seeing Oa° under the Municipal Corporations' Act the business of the Council for the year was over, and there were :neither Committees nor sub-Cont" mittees. We do not presume to question the accuracy of this ruling,but surely if the resolutions were inadmissible, then in the first place their pro- poser should have been made cognisant of the fact before they were printed on the agenda. It is an insult to the Council to issue an agenda contain- ing business which it has no power to transact. In the second place His Worship the Mayor shoold not have allowed the discussion to be opened who he knew that the whole thing was futile. There was no reason that we could see, to await the raising of a mysterious point of order by Conecillor Clegg. And finally, it was a mistaken indul: gence on the part of the Mayor to have presided over an informal, aimless, and heated debate, which resulted in nothing but an unpleasant SCentl' So far as the q•iestion at issue was raised it did not give rise to any presumption that the l'Jwe Clerk would submit the statement of the case to either the Investigation Committee or the Council' He denied point blank that he had ever given ill undertaking or promise to do so. As we have the fullest confidence in the accuracy of our report which was quoted by Councillors Lord,Pembertoh, and Stott on Tuesday night, we can only presoo!! that at the October meeting the Town Clerk inag,e a very unfortunate slip of the tongue. Not onlY</p>
        <p>does cair report credit him with a distinct promise to go through the statement of the case with the Committee, but the great majority of the mem- bers present understood that this was so. The Position of affairs now does not tend to disarm suspicion by any means. Why should there b !uch an objection to the statement of the case, If prepared by the Town Clerk, being placed before the Committee which investigated the charges ? Why too should such ingenious excuses as that the Committee ceased to exist when it reported, be formulated ? All this only tends to foster the public impression that a very serious !natter is being deliberately kept in the dark, and it would surely be to the interests of all concerned that a more candid policy should be adopted, The Town Clerk might,without any loss of dignity, relax the rigour of his professional exclusiveness nd give whatever ocular or other demonstration is required to assure the committee that the preamble and affidavits as submitted to the Attorney General are as complete as they desire.</p>
        <p>It is almost unique TEMPERANCE AND to find a Corporation PUBLIC HEALTH, interesting itself in temperance propa- ganda and the Preston Health Committee have set an example which might, with advantage, b 3 followed by other municipalities. A handbill is to be printed and delivered to each householder in the borough pointing out the evils attending the excessive use of alcohol. It may be objected that such work is outside the sphere of a health authority, but there can be no gainsaying the fatthat temperance must have its effect for good upcm the public health. If a Corporation therefore con- ceives it to be a matter of duty to urge any measures which conduce to a healthy community it is vite within its rights in taking such steps. The innovation of the Preston Health Camraitte is worthy of emulation and we would commend the idea to the consideration of our own " City Fathers."</p>
        <p>As Liberals we are THE ELECTIONS. chastened. All three official Liberal nominees were defeated at the polls on Wedne4- day, and in one instance a retiring Councillor, Mr. Peacock, failed to secure re-slection. This is the loss of a seat, although his Non-Trading opponent is understood to be a Liberal in politics. In Howley Ward a Tory Non-Trader has succeeded R retired Liberal Non-Trader after a severe contest With an official Liberal in Mr. Pickavance. Town Hall Ward has retained its Non-Trading representative, Councillor Benson, in preference to :Air. James Critchley, who flew the Liberal fla,r. It is obvious, we think, that as Liberals we made an error of judgment in endeavouring to raise a political issue in the three wards. Our hereditary foes, ths Tories, were more wily and refused to be drawn, with the result that the Non-Traders had the field practically to themselves and with— out hesitation placed themselves in opposition to the Liberal party. Their unmistakable triumph only makes it the more regrettable that Liberals and Non-Traders should be in Opposition. With such an object as purity in government all true Liberals must be in sympathy, and we are at a loss to understand why the No Trading ideal ideal should not be one of the Libor il ideals. It will certainly be a regrettable and unprofitable circumstance if Liberals and Non • Traders as parties are henceforth to be openly antagonistic. As individuals they cannot be dis. sociated, and we trust that SOM3 basis of under- standing may he possible which will obvisee a repetition of Wednesday's unfortunate contre- temps. NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>Pickavance has not been elected so that there ja small hope of the road and street-paving in the Borough being much improved yet awhile. Has Mr. Austin really done any serious harm? Seeing that, since Lord Salisbury's jocular appoint- ment of him in 1896,he has blossomed forth " Times", after " Times," there is very little of his verse which ,fortnnately, has ever really haunted the public.— Bystander." *</p>
        <p>- * - [ Sir John Bninner, Bart., M.P., has appointed a ' usical director to assist those of the employees of -Ilrunner, Mond and Co.'s works at Winnington and :Sandbach, Cheshire, who wish to cultivate their taste ,for music. There is nothing like big dividends for 'Making utilitarian projects easy of accomplishment. * * The Wesleyans of Stockton Heath are to be con- gratulated on having at last realised their expectations . and entered into possession of a new church. We hope that in addition to ftwthering the Kingdom of . God it will do much towards adjusting the balance of denominational power in the district. Eor years the 'Church (with a capital C) has dominated village affairs 'and it is only quite recently that Non-conformity has teen able -to raise its head. There is a notabl6 „broadening of thought and an extended toleration in .Stockton Heath to-day, and we hope that it will -eventually leave the chrysalis stage and emerge into the open as an enlightened, democratic, and Christian : community.</p>
        <p>* ** Eminently plain-spoken and practical were the addresses hy the Rev. E. Lloyd-Jones at the new Stockton Heath Wesleyan Church on Thursday. He is a man fully alive to the shortcomings of twentieth- century Christianity, and he does not, like some pharisaical preachers, imagine that all the fault lies with other denominations and churches. The ; 'Wesleyan Methodist Church is not all that it should be, and Mr. Lloyd Jones drove the fact home in no uncertain fashion. He is concerned for the Methodist . Church which, be says, "taken circuit for circuit, church for church, and man for man is not worth one half as .• lunch to the Kingdom of God as it was when I was a ; boy." This is a grave indictment from within • and should give pause to all Methodist7workers. Some of the lines on which Christian effort should be expended • were plainly indicated by the preacher—first and fore- , most being the breaking down of anocial barriers and distinctions in the House of God, and making it the Mecca of the masses. * 5 * • Councillor Pemberton and the other Non-Trading Councillors took an unprecedented course at the Council meeting when the vote of thanks to the Mayor for his services in the chair was proposed, and we are . inclined to deprecate their action. We have differed from His Worship on many occasions, and we have sided with the Non-Traders in some of their differ- ences with him, but at such a time as the present we •.; are firmly of opinion that differences should be sunk : • and only the hest that a man has done be recollected. -A.-Merman Bolton, with all his deficiencies,as the pre- siding genius of the Council and Chief Magistrate of the Borough has put in an enormous amount of 11nle and labour during his term of office, and for that , '• °e is entitled to the thanks of the community. It should be remembered that the office of Mayor is no Sinecure and that in Warrington the sweets of office are Practically non-existent. A very considerable Personal sacrifice is demanded of the occupant of our Mayoral chair in these tronblons times, and We regret that this much at least has not beeen unanimously recognise.l- -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>DEATH OF MR. PETER STUBS.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. PETER STUBS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Peter Stubs, :LP., of Illaisdon 1-fall, Gloucester- 'shire, and formerly of Warrington. passed away at his lesidence on Sunday from congestion of the lungs and ileart failure. Mr. Stubs. who was seventy-five years , ac'e, was chairman of the firm of Messrs. Peter ' sub's,i, Ltd.. file and tool merchants, and formerly Zaided at Statham Lodge. Lymm. At, one time Mr. tuhs %vas a familiar and prominent figure in the corn- ercial life of Warrington, but since the firm in which e Was a partner was turned into a limited company .rifteen years ago, he has resided at blaisdon Hall, Gloucestershire. Mr. Stubs did not take an active part in public work in Warrington, but he ?las ever ready to nssist all charitable movements WRS one of the oldest members of the Magisterial ,e,acb and was also a justice of the peace for Lanes- ire, Gloucestershire and Cheshire. He was widely hUOWO as a very successful breeder of shire horses and a most up-to-date stud farm at Blaisrlon. During :91 residence in Warrington he made a wide circle of Penda and was on every hand highly respected, and ;11 death will be deeply regretted. The funeral took Tana on Wednesday at Blaisdon, the Rev. E. Douglas officiating. There was a large attendance of 1505 and former friends and on every hand genuine et Wee evidenced. rae jerence.s to the late Mr. Stubs were made at the "me and of the Warrington Town Council on Tuesday, b— etore the commencement of the business at the "urrington County Petty Sessions on Wednesday.</p>
        <p>CRISP AND CREAMY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Notice. The Warrington " Examiner" is now published on Friday afternoon, price one penny, and is on sale at the various newsagents at about 4 o'clock. Should any difficulty be experienced in getting the paper the proprietors will undertake to deliver a copy at any address in Warrington by 5 o'clock on Friday after noon without extra charge. Address all communica- tions to "Examiner Office," Bewsey-street Warrington</p>
        <p>ASK for Msxmonshes Mon-Mesh MINERAL</p>
        <p>WATERS.—Advt. The Fatal Affray _ at Sankey.—Prlsoners</p>
        <p>Discharged. At the Warrington Occasional Court this (Friday) morning John Laheen, Patrick Noone, Michael Garrity, and Thomas Cuniff, Irish farm labourers, were charged on remand with the wilful murder of John Flemming, at .Sankey on the 15th October.—No evidence was offered against the prisoners who were accordingly discharged. LADIES' BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES, TINDER. THE CLOCK. SKID GE-STREET.AdVt. Crosfleld's Male Voice Choir Concert. A. concert arranged by the members of the Male Voice Choir connected with Messrs. Crosfield's works, I was held in the works assembly room on Tuesday evening. The proceedings attracted a large attendance and were of a very enjoyable character. A. c ipital programme was contributed to by Mr. W. H. Jackson (piano), Miss Eva Woodland, Misses Pooley, Mr. Cr. Hackney, Miss F. Leicester, Mr. E. Pownall, Mr. R. Osborne, Mr. W. Tynan, Mr. J. P. Hignett, Mrs. Adams and Miss Evans accompanists. The choir also rendered several selections in best style. H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC takes away that tired feel n e.—(Advt.) The Free Trade League. , The first of a series of public meetings promoted by the Warrington branch of the Free Trade League was held in St. Bardabas' schoolroom on Thursday evening. There was a fair attendance, Mr. S. Rigby Armitage, J.P., presiding. The principal spe iker was Mr. T. R. Canabell, of Manchester. and others present on the platform were Councillor James Evans Messrs. A. Rathbone, F. R. Roberts and Joseph Elwell. Owing to pressure on our space, we are compelled t bold over until next week any report of the speeches. PUBLIC BENEFIT. LTNDTCR TILE CLOCK Fort BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Oddfellowship. The fortnightly' meeting of the " Royal Friendship" Lodge, of Warrington District, of the National inde- pendent Order of Oddfellows, was held on Monday evening at the Blue Lion Inn, Market-place, when there was a good attendance of members and visiting brethren. N.G.I. Green was in the chair. After the ordinary business was over Bro. Bramhall presented a framed emblem of the order to PG. Henry Branwood for the able manner in which he had gone through the , various offices in the Lodge. Bro. Branwood thanked the members for their kindness in assisting him to go through the chairs of the lodge. Congratulatory speeches were delivered by Bros. Dickinson, Bramhall Litton, and:Pea cock, UNDER THE CLOCK for Good Boors.—Advt.</p>
        <p>St. John's Literary and Social Society. The usual fortnightly meeting of this Society was held in St. John's Church schoolroom on Tuesday evening, when the chair was taken by Mr. J. Bennett, hon. secretary. The proceedings were in the form of a " Hat Night," or series of impromptu debates. A large number of topics were put forward for discus- , sion, including the following, viz. :—" Do P.S.A. meetings do good?" "Are the people of Russia justi- fied in using force to obtain their freedom ? " "Should ministers take part in politics ? " " Are bazaars desir- able methods of raising money for Church purposes?" "Is the sermon a necessary part of public worship ? " &amp;e., &amp;e. The questions were as thoroughly discussed as was possible in the time available, and the meeting concluded with a vote of thanks to the chairman, it being acknowledged that the occupant of the chair had a somewhat responsible and difßcult task to perform on a" Hat Night." H. M. AsuroN's LIFE TONIC takes away that tired feeling.—(Advt.) Warrington P.S.A. Society. There was a good attendance at the meeting of the Warrington P.S.A. Society which was held in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street, on Sunday. Mr. Whitmore, who presided, drew attention in the course of a short address to the great need there was that steps should be taken to put down Sunday trading in Warrington. Mr. E. Wynne-Jones gave the address. He made an earnest appeal to all present to consecrate themselves to Christian work, and to take an interest in all that concerned the welfare of the community. Mr. J. H. Corin rendered two solos, "The Prodigal Son" and "The Toilers," Mr. B. Wadsworth accom- panying. Councillor Clegg and Mr. R. Nelson also took part in the meeting. One new member was wel- comed, making a total of 20 new members since the first Sunday in October,</p>
        <p>Asir for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINNRAL WAS.—Advt. Independent Order of Rechabltes.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of Charity Tent No.l was held in the tent room, Friars-green school on TuPsday. Bro. T. Moores, 0.K., presided, supported by the tent officers, there being a fairly good attendance of mem- bers present. The minutes of last meeting and the executive's report were read and adopted. 'Two new members were nominated and one new member initiated. It was proposed to send two delegates to represent the tent at the conference of the Lancashire districts to be held in the Central Hall, Manchester, on Saturday, November 4th. A ballot for the delegates resulted in Bros. W. Lowe and D. Hamblett being elected. An address should have been given in the muncipalisation of the drink trade by Bro. J. Bellian, D.E„ of Manchester, but he was unable to be psesent owing to being engaged in fighting the Bradford Ward,: Manchester, in the interests of Labour and Temperance.—Councillor Bro. F. Stott kindly con- sented at the last moment to introduce the subject. In a short but interesting address he dealt, with the evils of the drink monopoly, and explaineet both the strong points and the weak points of municipalisation. He was decidedly against the profits made from the trade being used to relieve the rates of the municipality, but advocated that municipal bowling greens, music halls and parks should be built for the benefit of the people instead. Bros. F. Merlin and Wright spoke against municipalis- ation and in favour of prohibition for the State and total abstinence for the individual and were of opinion that municipalisation of the drink trade would not decrease the evils of drinking but would make the control of the drink trade even more difficult than at present. A vote of thanks was proposed to Bro. Fred Stott for his able address by Bro. F. Merlin and seconded by Bro. W. Higginsnn, which brought a pleasant meeting to a close about 10 p.m. KITCHEN RANGES, Fire Grates, Tiles &amp;c., large etock.—.J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—ADvT.</p>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S CREAM CRACKERS</p>
