      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>Meeting of Druids.</head>
        <p>Meeting of Druids.</p>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. Rugby Football. ____ • [By " Ovalist."l Moat of Warrington's supporters are not sorry that the club is out of the Lancashire Cup Competition. With Fish and Dickenson on the injured list, they had practically no chance of winning the trophy, and the i anguish Of a defeat in the second round s mach less than it would have been in the senai-final or final. The chief regret is that Leigh should have beaten Warrington at Wilderspool again, and this time in a cup-tie. They are prone to be cocky at Leigh, and by this they will be deeming themselves quite War- rington's equals in a football sense—which they are not. Had both teams been fully represented on Satur- day instead of only one, the Loigh men would have got such a lesson as would have lasted them many a long day. On the run of the play on Saturday they deserved to win, but they were lucky in obtaining the deciding point at a time when the home forwards losoked like pushing them through the pavilion.</p>
        <p>The (rand Master of the Order of Druids (Bro. David Houghton) acclmpanied by P.G.M. Mellor of Crewe, paid an official visit to the Mermaid Lodge of the Warrington district on Saturday. After the ordinary Lodge business had been disposed of the Grand Master addressed the meeting. He was pleased to say that most kindred societies were progressing, and it was hoped that they had passed the corner of depression which had been responsible for the results of last year's working. Bro. Mellor, who ticeived an ovation, urged the members to take an active part in the business of the lodge, as being a duty they owed to themselves as well as their society. A hearty vote of thanks was given to Bro. Melhir for his presence, and his interesting address. A similar vote was heartily accorded to the Grand Master.</p>
        <p>PUBLTC BENEFIT.—IINDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCA.SHIRE.—Advt. Confirmation Service. The Bishop of Liverpool conducted a Confirmation service at Holy Trinity Church on 'Pneaday afternoon when 73 candidates —47 females and N males—were presented. Addressing the candidates, Dr. Chavasse said they must never forget their cNnfirmation. The confirmation meant to them that they had made a solemn promise to follow God, to speak as He spoke and to act as He acted. They should try never to speak a word which theywould not like God to hear,and never do an action which thy would not like him to see, and never have a thought which they would not like Him to know. They had promised not only to follow Christ, but to fight for Him as they were His soldiers. He expected them to fight His battles in Warrington, and fight for the right against the wrong, and love against hatred, malice, envy and strife. Popular Concert at Bold street. There was a good attendance at the second of the series of popular penny concerts held in Bold-street Wesleyan Schools on Saturday evening. The Rev. Joseph Manning presided. The programme, which was a highly enjoyable one, npened with a pianoforte solo by Mr. A. Lee. Mrs. Arthur Booth delighted the audience with her artistic interpretations of "Severed" and " Star of the Twilight." Each of these items called forth an encore and in response Mrs. Booth gave "Annie Laurie" and "Allan Water." Miss M. Gandy very pleasingly sang "A May mornings' and "My own folk' and was also recalled. Mr. (1. Gough was heard to advantage in" The Young Royalist" and "Asleep in the deep," and Mr. Radcliffe gave "I'll sing thee songs of Araby "and "Guiding star "in a creditable manner. Mr. Frank Hickman created considerable amusement with several humorous songs, and also acted as accompanist. During the interval a short address was delivered by Mr. W. Hickman who heartily commended the object of the concerts. WASHTNG AND WRINGTNG MACHINES try J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55 Bridge-street, Warrington.— ADVT. Sunday School Teachers in Conference The qnarterly meeting of the Warrington and Dis- trict Sunday School Teachers' Association was held on Monday evening in St. Barnabas' Schools the Rev. J. H. G. Hollingworth p-ceiling over a good attendance.—The Rev. P. L Adams, secretary of the North-West District of the Additional Cantos Society, ; read a Raper on " The teacher the friend of his scholars. Every teacher, be said, must have in some degree his scholar's welfare at heart, anc, try to bring him nearer to God. They should teach their scholars to reverence God and to love their C lurch. Many children lived in the midst of degradation and vice, and others lived in houses where no control was exer- cised. The home life in some cases, was a very great bar to Sunday school work. The way in which the teacher could get to know the kind of life the children led was by visiting their parents, which was very little done now. It was a more difficult work to teach the elder scholars, but perhaps easier to befriend them. They, perhaps, were being influenced by bad companions, and they therefore required more spiritual help. They should be urged to attend the Holy Com- munion regalarly, and then they would get a grasp of what spirituality meant. (Applause).— The Rev. E. C. E. Carleton said they were very grateful to Mr. Adams for giving such an excellent address, and he quite agreed with what he had said.—The Revs. H. R. Wild, W. Pollock-Hill, and the Chairman also thanked Mr. Adams for his address. Warrington Band of Hope Union The Rev. J. S. Hughes presided over the annual meeting of the Warrington Bind of Hope Unioa which was held at Gregory's Cafe.—The rep,wt of the Executive Committee, which was approved, showed the Union was in a very healthy financial condition, and that some gout work had been accomplished. Much anxiety had been caused the committee by the reports furnished by several societies as to the lack of sympathy they had experienced at the hands of the ministers, stewards and wardens of the places of worship to which they were connecte 1. In tho case lof some societies their meetings were never brightened ;by the presence of the ministers or chief laymen. In ' other cases the' societies were charged a heavy rent for the use of the room in which their meetings were held, time result of which was, that these societies were in a constant state of indebtedaess, many workers became discouraged, and it was exceed- ingly difficult to find officers to carry on the work. IThe Committee trusted that this state of aff tirs would soon be a thing of the past. and that every church would recognize that an efficient Band of If goo ov quite as essential as a Sunday school. The officers were elected as follows : —President, Mr. R. A. Naylor; treasurer, Mr. R. P. Williams ; hon. secretary, Mr. F. Gandy ; hon. assistant secretaries, Mr. Harry Woolley, and Mr. D. Hallett; auditors, Messrs. W. Wright and George Whitfield ; represen- tatives to the Lancashire and Cheshire Band of Hope Union, Messrs. Joseph Otven and F. Crandy.—lt was decided to promote a social for Band of Hope workers early in the winter.</p>
        <p>The game was of the usual cup-tie order, though by no means uninteresting. In the first half Leigh were clearly the better team, and seeing how dis- organized the home backs were they would have done better to open out the game instead of kicking for touch at every opportunity. Leigh might have had no wings at all instead of two speedy •men like Johnson and Neville for all the Use they were put to. Consequent upon the negative policy of the visiting halves, the visitors were only a dropped goal ahead at half-time. On resuming the home forwards asserted themselves and by really magnificent effsrts they kept Leigh almost wholly on the defensive. The rear men had chances galore, but what with the absenee of Fish and Dickenson, the poor form of Jordan anll the unfitness of Kenyon for such a struggle, they gave a heartbreaking display. Isherwdod and Harris alone did good work and they were well watched. Still, when Preston equalised by a fine goal, the odds were on the homesters. Then came Leigh's luck. Kenyon made a sad blunder and left a loophole in the defence which Johnson speedily took advantage of and won the match. I don't think Leigh would have got a try any other way, but it sufficed.</p>
        <p>As I have already indicated, the fine work of the forwards was the oily-bright spot in Warrington's display. Hallam re appeared at full back, and while he made some brilliant saves he was not the Hallam of old in fielding and kicking. But for Preston's ability in this direction Warrington must have been badly left in the punting department. The horns halves were not at their best by any means and in general smartness they had to play second fiddle .to Dunbavin and Whittaker. Perhap3 their worst fault was in tactics. At one time, when the forwards were controlling the game within five yards of the Leigh line, the Warruanteu halves passed out to Harris on the narrow side of the field three times in strocef- Rion. He had not a ghost of a chance of getting in, while there were endless possibilities on the open side. Surely, we have a right to expect.. better judgment than this from men like Hockenhall and Brooks ? Warrington conspicuously failed to 'take advantage of real opportunities or they would hive won.</p>
        <p>The semi-finals for the Lancashire Cup have now ti be fought out by Oldham and Wigan, and Leigh and Widnes, on the ground of the first named. Probably we shall see Oldham and Le;gh in the final, and with all respect to the latter, I 'oak upm the championship as a sweet thing far Oldham:</p>
        <p>Among Leigge nraches on Siturliy, the most in- teresting encounter was at Runcorn :.where the Linnets had to put up with their initial defeat at the hands of Swinton. They were unfortnaste in losing the services of Houghton, and as the score was only two points to nil, this accident prob Ably decided the issue. As a result Runcorn have now been • deposed from the leadership of the League.</p>
        <p>The Llagoe tahlo up-to-dlte is as follows For. Aga. Per-</p>
