        <head>Examiner, Saturday, November 4, 1905.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, November 4, 1905.</p>
        <p>EXTRAORDINARY COUNCIL INCIDENT.</p>
        <p>NON-TRADERS OBJECT TO VOTE OF THANKS TO THE MAYOR.</p>
        <p>MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>RESULTS OF THE THREE CONTESTS AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Close Struggle In Bswsey Ward.</p>
        <p>TOWN HALL WARD.</p>
        <p>William Beason (N.T.) 389 James Critchley (L.) Majority ' 131</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>And Leave the Council Chamher.</head>
        <p>And Leave the Council Chamher.</p>
        <p>In extraordinary incident °enured at the close of he meeting of the Warrington Town Council on Tuesday evening. The meeting was the last of the municipal year and the customary vote of thanks to the Mayor was proposed and seconded. Councillor Pemberton objected to the resolution on the ground that the Mayor had not displayad " unvarying courtesy and impartiality " to the Non-Traders. Councillor Lord was about to supplement his remarks when the Mayor declared that he was n ,t going to sit there to be insulted. Four Non-Trading members thereupon left the room and the resolution was I carried by the remaining Councillors with one dissentient. Alderman FRANcoNtu moved the resolution as follows :—" That the best thanks of the Council be pre- sented to William Bolton. Esq., for the satisfactory manner in which he has discharged the responsible duties devolving upon him as Mayor and chief magis- trate of the borough during this his second year of office, and for the unvarying courtesy and impartiality displayed by him when presiding over the deliberations of the Council audio his intercourse with its members." It gave him the greatest pleasure to propose the resolu- tion, because the Mayor had had a most ditlicult task to perform during the last year. Ho trusted that the Mayor's corning term of o Roe would he roach happier than the one now ended had been. They were all agreed that he had done his very best for the interests of the town. Alderman TENNIoN seconded. He endorsed the remarks of Alderman Francomb that the NI iyar hal pit in nr3re time than any of his predecessors,and at the same time he had done more talking. (Laughter.) He hoped, it would be many years before they hi a Mayor as loquacious as the present one. (Laughter.) However, Alderman Bolton richly deserved the vote of thanks. Personally, he had crossed him on many occasions, and he had differed with hirn,hut although the Mayor had per haps been somewhat rough in his manner he had always found him endeavourin. to mete out justice to every- body. He hoped that Siring his next year of ()Mee the , members would give him all the assistance they could , to enable him to conduct the debates of .the C.3unci I . . .</p>
        <p>with something like the dignity the proceedings should he conducted. If the members wonld give the Mayor more support than they had done there w.suld not be so many unpleasant scenes like those which had taken place during the last six months. He hp ) 1 that in the coming year the members of the Council and the occupant of the chair w.mld have pirrtsanter and hapnier time, and that the debates would be conducted a little more gentlemanly, and ii a manner that would do credit to the fair name of the town of Warrington. Alderm In SNINITIMRST supported the motion. He wished to ms.'ce an addition to the wording. tie thlnght the resolution should include. the Mayoress.. (Hear, . .</p>
        <p>„ hear.) He felt sure they were all :no ,el indebted to the I Councillor CHANTLER thanked all who had supported .1 Mayor for the way in which he had conducted the him and said he also wished those who had not sup- business. He had been a bit rough somitimes, anl had . ported him to rettplinber that he was now the member on several occasions " got his rag out" by stopping I for the ward. (A Voice : " Yes, and you were never him (the soeaker),but notwithstanding that, he was very any good"). Anyhow, he would do his best for the pleased that the Mayor had had health awl streogta to I ward and also for the town and he thought that in conduct the business of the Council. The position was twelve months the interrupter would have altered his no light one for any public man, and considering whit I opinion. (Cheers.) the Mayor had accomplished during the last term of Mr. PM:A:VANCE said he was the defeated candidate olace—there had been the matter of the Ship Cinal and', for Howley Ward. He woo sorry the intelligence of other qoestions—he was surprised that he had been able I Howler did not go so far as to place him at the head of to stand it. He had attended meetings both in the i the poll. (Hear, hear, and laughter.) He would say town and in London, and the whole thing came out !nothineagainot his opponent. Councillor Chantler was quite successful They often referred to the position ;a centlernae and they hart hod a good honest fight. He of Mayor as being honourable ; it was honoorable, awl (Mr. Pickavance) had had to contend with everything— there was is great deal of work attached to it, and to Conservatism, Radicalism, Socialism and also the Non- the Who'll's, too. He was sure if some of their friends , Traders. He had always had a leonine in favour of the knew the amount of money some of the oflicials savod Non-Traders, but after that night it was war to the during 12 months they would not talk as they did. knife and " the Non-Traders could go to the d---" S