mount of sympathy with their work. Mit now as general manager of a public concern. It had been a it had got to personalities and he should do his utmost painful duty to sit there and try to carry on discussions to thwart their efforts. In conclusion Mr. Pickavance when gentlemen had such a way of making insimin- said he did not intend to seek the saxifrages, of the tions and recriminations, and then be insulted by those Howley Ward electors again. who knew what he had done in the only way they had A vote of thanks was accorded Mr. Pickavance for of paying hint hack—that or giving him a backhanded contesting the Ward, on the proposition of Mr. J cut, which was not worthy of consideration. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>MENOM</p>
        <p>WOODS, seconded by Mr. VO,,E, and supported by Mr. HANIBLErr.</p>
        <p>Mr. GEORGEFALLINGTON delivered a brief address of thanks to the Town Hall Ward workers, and said he defied Mr. Dodds or anyone else to pint a finger at their candidate. He urged all present to keep in view the Parliamentary contest, which might come at no very distant date. Mr. CRITCHLEY thanked his workers, and complained of the apathy of many electors in not voting. He trusted that in the future they would be able to find a stronger candidate who would win the ward for the honour of the Liberal party. A vote of thanks was accorded to Mr. Critchley on the motion of Mr. O'BniEs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>BEWSEY WARD.</head>
        <p>BEWSEY WARD.</p>
        <p>James Dickenson (Ind. N.T ) Peter Peacock (L.) '327</p>
        <p>Majority</p>
        <p>110 WLE Y WARD.</p>
        <p>T. Chantler (C N.T.) 402 El Pickavance (L.)</p>
        <p>Majority 94</p>
        <p>Although there were only three contests for muni- cipal honours at Warrington on Wednesday interest in the e4ections was very considerable. The wards in which the electors were called upon to make a choice of a representative were Town Hall, Bewsey and Howley and the non-trading question was again one of the main issues. The most exciting contest was witnessed in Bewsey Ward where the return of Mr. Peter Peacock, the retiring Liberal representati.e, was opposed by Mr. James Dickenson who came forward as an:lnde- pendent Non-Trader and was victorious by a narrow majority. Another keen struggle took place in Rowley Ward where Mr. E Pickavancepand Mr.Thos.Chantler (C), the last named of whom had the support of the Non-Trading Association, were the candidates. There was a large and enthusiastic band of workers on each side, but although Mr. Pickavance made a sturdy fight, he was defeated. In Town Hall Ward, Mr. William Benson, the retiring Non-Trading Councillor, had as his Opponent Mr. James Critchley (I.) but succeeded, in retaining his seat by an enlarged majority.</p>
        <p>AT THE POLLING STATIONS. The day could hardly have been more unfavourable for the elections. Rain fell almost continuously and the conditions were most depressing. The workers, however, did not relax their efforts and although during the day-time polling WA; very slow at nearly all the stations, there was a rush of voters in the evening. The polling stations for the Town Hall Ward were at Kendrick-street School. Mr. W. H. J. Jenkins and Mr.</p>
        <p>Edwin Bradshaw were the presiding officers, and gr. D. Skelton and gr. S. Wright poll clerks. The number of electors is 958. In 139wsey Ward the polling booths were at 83. Hoyle- street and St. Ann's School. Dannett-street. The pre- siding officers were Mr. J. R. Williams and gr. Roger Pierpoint, and the poll clerks Mr. H. Essenhigh and Mr. W. T. Crank respectively. In this ward there rto:3 802 electors. The National School, Chnrch-strest, was the polling station for Howley Ward. gr. J. Houghton and Mr. T. EL Spinks were the presiding o:ficers, and gr. W. B. Randall, and gr. T. J. Hopkins the pall clerks. The number of electors is 1,128. COUNTING THE VOTES. The polling stations were clesedat 8 o'clock and Ithe ballot boxes were conveyed to the Town Hall where the votes were counted in the Council chamber. At about 8.45 the results were declared as above by the presiding aldermen—Alderman Smethurst (Town Hall ‘Vtrd), Alderman Fairclongh (Bscvsey Ward), Alderman Francomb (Rowley Ward). , A vote of thanks was accorded to the presiding alder- man for Town Hall Ward on the proposition of Coun- cillor Benson seconded by :Mr. Critchley. Alderman S E rit M/S r,in responling, said it had been a greit pleasure to him that the election in the ward had been so peaceably fought and be hoped they would all be good friends after it. Councillor DICK EVSON moved and Mr. P. Peacock seconded a vote of thanks to Alderman Pairclough who briefly acknowledged it. In moving a similar compliment to Alderman Fair. dough, Conncillor Chantler said they had quite a " happy family" at Howley, the best of fooling being shown during the election, :Mr. PICK &amp;VANCE seconded and said he never met a more genial or better fellow to fight than Mr. Chantler. They had been quite " pals " together. He was quite satisfied with the fight—he was beaten by 94. He did not wish to raise any discordant note, because he had had such a frienlly fight, but he honestly hoped that before many yetrs were over the Non-Traders would think twice before they said again what they htd said against him. Alderman Fitavooma returned thanks. SPEEEEIES FROM THE TOWN HALL STEP</p>
        <p>A large crowd assembled to hear the declaration of the results front the Town Hill steps, aril the figures were receive I with cheers from the successful candi- d ttes' supporters. Councillor DICZCENSON stid he desired, with all his heart, to thank the electors of Bewsey ‘Vard for having rallied round him and returned him. The light had been a rather noisy one, but so far as he and his opponent were concerned, it had been a comfortable one, and they had got on very well together. He promised that he would do what, ho could for the god of the ward and of the town. (Cheers). Couniillor BENISON briefly thanksl the burgesses of Town Hall Ward.</p>
        <p>WINN</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>ST. JAMES'S BAZAAR. PRETTY SCENE IN THE SCHOOLROOM</head>
        <p>ST. JAMES'S BAZAAR. PRETTY SCENE IN THE SCHOOLROOM</p>
        <p>A very pretty scene is presented to visitors to St. James' Scholl, Old-road, Latohford, where a four days' bazaar in aid of several worthy parochial objects, was opened on Wednesday afternoon. The room is gay with many-coloured flags and streamers, and the well- laden stalls—which bear eloquent testimony to the efforts of the lady workers—are embellished with light, decorative materials which are most tastefully arranged. The promotion of the bazaar has been entered into with great esthasitsm. and the event promises to be a complete success Briefly stated ths objects are renovating and cleaning St. James' Church; (2), freeing St. Hilda's Mission from debt ; (3), making certain necessary improvements to the schools (4), repairing the church tow.,r(work already done). The total amount required is £6OO and the congregation are putting forward every effort to raise this sum. The bazaar officials, to whom credit is due for their willing • labour, are :---Chairman, Rev. H. B. Firth. hi A.: Hon. Secretaries,Rev. D. R. Davies. 8.A., and Mr. W. H Stansfieid, M.A.; Hon. Treasurer, Mr. C. S. Burgess, J. P. ; Hun. Secretaries for entertainments. Messrs. Brayshsw and Kertiand. THE STALL-HOLDERS. Tic E CON'GREGATIONAT.STALL:—Mesiarnes Carrnthers, Dickson, Gibson, Mee, Misses Gibson and A. Parry; deputies, Mesdames Acton and Hutchinson, Misses Bolton, Parry and Peers ; as.istants, Mesdames Davies, Dowling, Harris, and Smith, Misses M. Bolton, E. Bolton, Bowcock. D. Bowcock, Jefferson and McDowell. TRE MlsszrOv STALL :—MesdArnes Denoett, Foster, Hughes and Tango ; Misses Isherwood, Rhodes and TOnge. Tim Dtv Settoot. STALL :—Mr. Stansflold, Arisses Plinston, and Taylor ; assistants, Airs. Grant, Misses Barlow, Darlington, Dackworth, Heywood, Hollings- worth, Marrow, Norton, Sims, Smallwood and Wright; Mr. George, Mr. Thomson, and Mr. 0. Sinclair. TICE SUND.AT ScHoOL STALL :—Misses Bowers, Dillon,Eekersley, B. Eckersley, Gibbs, Hancock, Highley, Heywood, Hughes, Livingstone, Spencer, Smith, Woodin and Wright ; Messrs. Crompton, Livingstone and Newton ; stall Secretary. Miss Burgess. PICTURE POST CARD STALL :—Miss H. and Master F. Stansfield.</p>
        <p>THR REFRE3IIMENT STALL :—Mesdames Fargher. Hallwood, Houghton, and Venn; assistants, Mesdames Booth, Calvert, Jefferson, Kenyon, Lingard, Pierpoint and Ray. Nfisqes Bailer and &amp;transom TILE FLOWER STALL:—Ihe Misses Broadbent. Tug HARDWARE STALL ...-111iss Darbyshiro. SWEETS AND CIGARETTE STALL :—Mrs. Chester and Miss Chester; assistants, Miss Millwood. TIIE VOCNG MEN's' STALL:—Messrs. .1. Grounds,E. Leigh, E. Mee, W. Toft and H. Warburton. TCE CRE k:%l STALL :—Mrs. Hilson. • BRAN Tun: —Miss V. Wood. BRAN Dr:—Aisies E. Dunba,vand and G. Hackney</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>Tqg opENING CERRMONY;</head>
        <p>Tqg opENING CERRMONY;</p>
        <p>The bazaar was onecei on Wednesday afternoon by Mrs. Harold Bros.dbent, in the absence through bereave- ment of Mrs Frost, who had promised t ) perform the ceremony. There was a large attendance, the Vicar (Rev. H. B. Firth' presiding. Amongst others present were the Rev. D R. Dtvies, Messrs. J. Fargher (vicar's warden), and J. Ecicersley (people's warden), Mr. C. S. Burgess (bazaar treasurer;, the sidesmen, lay readers, and stewards.</p>
        <p>The VICAR. In explaining the objects of the bazaar. said their first aim was to raise £3OO to beautify and decorate the church. Then the schools in which they were assembled had been declared by the local educa- tional authority to be in need of about £1.50, as they needed improving in various ways. Thirdly, the tower of the church, which was really of great service to the townspeople, as it contained an excellent clock. was last year declared to be danzerons, :411 £5O had to be spent upon it. Their mission church of St. Hilda, which was in a rapidly groving part of the parish, was in debt to the extent of nAerlyll.o9. What they hoped to raise by their effort was £6OO, which would put the 'parish in a sound financial condition He thought few bazaars which had been held in any town could boast of more kindly and loyal helpers than that bazaar had had. The Vicar expressed great regret that owing to the death of a near relative, Mrs. Frost, who had been announced to open the bazaar, was unable to attend. She had written sending a very kind donation. They felt very grateful to Mrs. Harald Broedbent, who had stepped into the breach &amp;al had consented to open the btzaar. (Applause.) • Mrs. HAROLD BROADBENT. who was cordially received said she was very sorry that Mrs. Frost could not attend: and especially for the cause which had kept her away The room looked perfectly charming and reflected great credit upon the etellholdere. The object of the bazaar was a very good one and she wished the m all possible SUCCOR! in their undertaking. (Applause.) A handsome bouquet was presented to Mrs. Broadbent by Miss Fargher. Mr. 0. 8. BURGESS moved a vote of thanks to Mrs. Broadbent and said that was not the first occasion when the members of Rt. James's congregation had had to thank her for her kiedeees. (Atmlause.) Mr. J. Farieuee seconded, and the motion having been heartily accorded, the bazar commenced. THURSDAY'S PROCEEDINGS. The bazaar was re-opened on Thursday by Mrs. Fielding•O 'td, of London, whose late husband wag ' formerly Vicar of the parish. The Rev. H. B. Firth again presided and those present on the platform included Mrs. Fielding-Gold, Miss Fieldinz-Ould, Mise Parry. the Rev. D. R. Davies. and Mr. T. E. Gibson. The VICAR said that no doubt some of those present would remember Mrs Fielding•Ould when she lived at the old Vicarage in Knutsford•road. and her late husband was the Vicar of the parish. Since that time many changes had taken place, one of the later developments belog that that part of the parish had been enriched by the erection of a mission, which was serving a very armful purpose. Both the Latchford and the Wilderspool parts of the parish had undergone quite a trens formation in the time he referred to, Wilderspool-causeway. which was then a muddy, country road, being now one of the finest streets in the town. It was delightful to think that Mrs. Fielding-Oold's interest in the parish was still main- tained—so mach so that she had undertaken the journey from London to Warrington solely with the object of opening the bazaar. (Applause.) Mrs. FIELDING•OCLD said no doubt many of those present would remember their old Vicar, the late Rev." Fielding-Oald, and she considered it a very happy coincidence that she ehoald have been asked to open that bazaar on All Souls Day,which many people set apart to remember friends to whom the messeee had already been sent, " Friend, go up higher." It was there- fore most appropriate to think of him who loved to work among them and with quick feet and ready heart to help any who were in trouble. Since th-n other good w)rkers shoal be loved had joined him. and the remembrance of their departed friends might help each .of them to to their little best. There were people who considered bazaars very unworthy means of raising money for a church, but surely an undertaking that could call forth so many unselfish virtues and that enabled the poorest and humblest to do something for the common cause should not be despised. (Applause. Miss VERA WOOI then presented Mrs. Fielding-Onld with a beautiful bouquet. Mr. T. E GIBSON moved a vote of thaeks to Mrs. Fielding-Oald and said they were moved to feelings of humiliation when they thought that the go )d work com- menced and carried on many years ago by Mr. Fielding-Ould had not been extended as mach as they would have liked or as it ought to have been. He hoped all present would do their utmost to farther the work of God is the parish by united efforts. (Applause.! The Rev. D. R. DAVIES seconded and the motion was cordially ogreed to.</p>
        <p>The VICAR announced that the amount taken at the bazaar on %traineelay was £135 17s. 51. The subscrip- tions received Tip to date amounted to £l2B, making a. total of £733 174. Si.</p>
        <p>The amount rea iz?,l by Taureds) 'a prooeedilgs wae £l7O.</p>
        <p>TFIE ENTERTAINMENTS. An excellent series of entertainments has sonsiderably enhanced the attractiveness of the bazaar. On Wednes- day evening two concerts were given by Mr. E. Davies's party, the programme including songs by Messrs. J. Bill and H. W. Turner, selections by a quartet party, a violin solo by Mr,Tom Boothroyd,A.l.U.sl.,and a piano- forte 8310 by Miss M. P. Gibson, L L.O.N. The accpm. panist was Mr. E. Davies. F.V.C.M. Another enjoyable feature was the amusing farce " Incompatibility of Temper," presented by the Misses Houghton and Rlystoa and Mr. W. J. Barnes. On Thursday evening songs and coatnine dances were given by Miss Sylvia Broadbent. Miss Margaret Broadbent, Mies Bleckly and Miss Wilicoolre and were much appreciated. This(Friday) evening a concert will be given by Miss B. Ecloraley and party. At intervals of the bazaar selections have been rendered by Miss Turner's band, and Miss Millwood and Miss, A. Fry have contributed pianoforte duets. There are numerous "aide shows," including abooting gallery, fish pond, and art gallery. FURTHER OPENING CEREMONIES. This (Friday)af ternoon the b is being opened by I Mr. Littler. J P., of Runcorn, and on Saturday Mrs. Powell, of Sewerby Vicarage, will perform the ceremony.</p>
        <p>MEM</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>Spence's Statement</head>
        <p>Spence's Statement</p>
        <p>Mr.</p>
        <p>Weakness—Severe Headaches and Indigestion cured by a Iron-Ox Tonic Tablets. Some months ago Mr. C. Spence of Laurel House, Ampleforth, suffered from a severe attack of Influenza. This left his syetem in a weakened con- dition, and he soon found himself a prey to indiges- tion ; the latter resulted in violent headaches, and</p>
        <p>pain and distress after eating, nausea and nervousness Naturally Mr. Spenoe was unable to give his daily duties proper attention. He felt wea k and unfit for work. He was always nervous—always tired. He heard that Iron-Ox Tablets were bringing health and happines3 to many men and women throughout the land and he determined to give this wonderful remedy a trial The following letter addressed to you tells in his own words how Iron-Ox Tablets clued his indigestion, banishedthis headaches, brought him health and strength.</p>
        <p>Laurel House, Amplefortb. I have derived great benefit from your Iron-0 Tablets. I was laid up with a severe attack of influenza some time ago, and after my recovery I suffered con- stantly from severe headaches and indigestion.. Your Iron-Ox Tablets have completely cured the indigestion. Since taking them I have had no trouble with head- aches—in fact, I have never felt better in my life. (Signed), C. SPENCE.</p>
        <p>Indigestion is the root of more sickness.more misery fore unhappiness than most people realise. If you digestive organs are working properly, if you are assimilating your food naturally, and obtaining proper nourishment from it, if your system is working regu- larly, and is being cleansed of all impurities, your nerves will be quiet, your blood will be pure, you will sleep soundly, you will get up in the morning refreshed and able to do a hard da3's work. You will not be so irritable, trifles will cease to trouble you, your life will be brighter. Won't you heed Mr. Spence's message ? Why not begin to-day to cure by safe and natural means the indigestion which is the root of all your troubles ? This statement from 31r. Spence should convince you that Iron-Ox Tablets can cure your indigestion, can remove that distress and nausea, can bring quiet nerves, sound sleep, good health. A Dainty Aluminium Pocket ,'acket of 50 Tonic Tablets for Is. If your chemist has not got them they will be sent post free for Is. by the Iron-Ox Remedy Co., Ltd., 20 Cockspur-street, London, S.W.</p>
