SMETHURST said he was sorry that the amendment had been moved. He did not think they would gain anything by taking spite out of people in that way. He was hoping that in a short time they would get Messrs.Fairclough to give £l,OOO and if they had to pull down the bridge and build a new one that would be something towards it. The question of the widening of the bridge had not been shelved as they were considering a scheme whereby they would prob- .-ably be able to erect the cantilever at less cost. He hoped they would not fall out with Nfesars.Fairclough. Councillor LORD : It is not a question of falling out it is a question of coming to terms. Alderman PIF.RPOINT thought the opposition to the Tesolution was not serious, and that the amendment had been moved simply to allow people to express their 'views before the Council. Councillor LORD withdrew his amendment. Alderman BESNF.TT : One hears frequent complaints of smells arising from the sewer in Chnrch-street and it is suggested that it is not periodically flushed. If it is going to be overloaded it will aggravate the nuisance which exists.</p>
        <p>Alderman TINNION in answer to Mr. Ashcroft said the matter had received his personal attention and was quite in order. With reference to the sewers in Church- street. If the committee could see their way to spend £15,000 or £20,000 they would deal with the sewer. The sewer was not over loaded but it certainly was loaded to its fullest capacity. The treatment of the sewage from the barracks in the manner suggested would relieve the Church-street sewer to a certain extent. The minutes were approved. THE RECEIPTS FOR THE CORPORATION BOWLING GREEN. The MAYOR in moving the confirmation of the minutes of the Town Hall, Park and Baths Committee referred to the resolution "that the Borough Surveyor report -upon the cost of removing the wall on the west and south-west sides of Queen's Gardens, and of substitut- ing a suitable palisading. " He did not agree with the suggestion that the wall should be pulled down as he did not think it would be any improvement. Alderman TINNION moved that the minute be con- firmed. The MAvort: I will withdraw it. Alderman TINNION thought it would be a gross waste of public money to pull the wall down. (Hear, hear.) Councillor PEMBERTON asked if the receipts from the Corporation bowling green mentioned in the minutes— £l4 4s. Id.—were for the whole of the season. The MAYOR: For the whole of the season up to the -end of September. DISPUTE NVITH THE LEIGH CORPORATION. At a meeting of the Water Committee on the 23rd October, the Town Clerk, Water Engineer, and Deputy- Chairman of the Water Committee of Leigh attended with reference to the expenses incurred by this com- mittee in supplying the Borough of Leigh with a temporary supply of water.</p>
