      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>CURE OF SKIN DISEASE.</head>
        <p>THE CURE OF SKIN DISEASE.</p>
        <p>Golden Rules for Every Householder.</p>
        <p>The skin is both a striking factor of beauty and an important excretory organ, carrying out of the body, through perspiration, effete and waste material that would inevitably prove fatal if allowed to remain. According to Sir Erasmus Wilson. the pores by which this perspiration is carried on average 3.523 to the square inch of skin surface. Krause puts the total nunibr over the whole body at 2,381,245. These glands, placed together, would make an orifice twice the size of the human mouth.</p>
        <p>Little wonder, therefore, that a diseased skin has a bad effect on the general health. Little wonder, too, that Zam-buk, the well-known skin-cure, by cleansing the pores and dispelling disease, is often said to improve bodily health at the fAITIO time. For in lun-buk it is conceded that we have a balm of rare richness and remarkable skin-renewing power. It is a preparation which ruts one in mind of the many excellent oils and balms used by the Grecian ladies of fashion in bygone days, who, if they took pride in anything, certainly bestowed every pain and attention to ensure a soft, smooth, and healthy skin. In recent years the anxiety to produce the beauty of skin that is only "skin deep,"—and not always that— has led to the introduction of ointments, salves, and embrocations which generally possess one or two grave objections.</p>
        <p>These common preparations are generally composed of rancid animal fats and mineral poisons ; and thoy I are useless either from the mere fact of their coarseness, and hence the inability of the skin to absorb them, or from the fact that their ingredients, if partially I absorbed, are of such a nature as to produce negative or even harmful results. Compounds of mercury, zinc,lead, and bismuth are examples of these risky and undepend- able preparations. Zara-Bak, on the other hand, is a refined balm, prepared direct from the juices and balsams of certain valuable medicinal herbs ; and the manner of the combination of these' juices is such as to develop their rare skin-curing and skin-healing proper- ties to a point neither attained nor attempted by any ordinary salve or ointment. Virtually a "skin-food," Zara-bilk, besides dispelling disease and regulating perspiration, actually promotes the growth of new and healthy tissue. It also imparts Ito the skin the "tone" which, in the damp foggy season, is so necessary if we are to escape from, or get better of, "chaps," rawness and cold-sores. The greatest successes of Zam-fluk have been in the cure of eczema ulcers, chafing, chapped hands, chilblains, sore feet, scalp diseases, ringworm, bad legs, piles, abscesses, inflamed patches and sores of all sorts, in reducing swellings, healing injuries, ending or preventing suppuration, and in alleviating the pains of rheumatism, sciatica, and lumbago. Chemists sell Zam-Buk in one-and-three- halfpenny and two-and-nine boxes (the quantity in the large size being equal to nearly four boxes of the lesser size); also for same prices direct from the Zam-buk Co., 4, Redcross-street, London, E.C. The perfect treatment for prevalent skin disease is the Zam-buk treatment; it lathe treatment embodying the right substance to rub with when Nature urges you to rub a place that hurts. Follow Nature and use Zam-buk ; such is the golden rule for every home.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, November 4, 1905.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, November 4, 1905.</p>
        <p>TALL RIGHTS RESERVED.]</p>
        <p>LOVE IN IVIIITECMOEL;</p>
        <p>HALLEVELL SUTCLIFFE (Author of" Rieroft of IF-Wiens," 4e.)</p>
        <p>, If the clothes that were hanging out in the toad in front had been washed, it had been done sa cleverly as to deceive the most microscopic vision ; there was a subtelty of concealment about it that spoke of a mind humble and in no way de- -4[0119 of parading its virtues before the world. rhere were also some children playing in the sutter a man leaning against a doorway, smok- ing a short, black pipe, and an emaciated mon- grel disappearing round the corner of the street, with a bone in its mouth— pursued by half a iozen urchins who were, if anything, a trifle more hungry than the dog, and who had a dim kind of feeling that the human had more right than the beasts that perish to any food that was going. And the afternoon sun was !oohing down and gibing, in a coldly dispassionate way, at the meanness and the squalor of it all. The sun grew red, then copper, and clouds came up like dirty smoke above the chimney pots ; the wind blew in peevish gusts, played awhile with the flotsam of the streets, then went down Use drear crying fitfully, like a child who cares no longer for its playthings. And the clouds dropped rain ; and the streets grew greasy as their denizens ; and over all was London rain, the weariest thing upon earth. A man stood looking out upon it all from the smeared window of a first-floor room —a room which was foreign, in all particulars, save that of grime, to the atmosphere of Whitechapel. There were rare prints on the walls, and traces of a fas- tidious taste in the selection and arrangement of the furniture. A half-empty bottle of whiskey stood upon the table, and beside it a ease of sur- gical instruments. On the imintel-piece, under the boating-groups, and the crossed oars and other trophies of old Oxford days, a girl's face looked down upon _the_scene. A winqome face it was, fresh with the beatify of illusion, and tender as a frolic wind of April ; it brought a whisper of country lanes, of honeysuckle hedgerows and primrose bordered ways, into this prison-11min whose gaolers were the unending hear Jess streets.</p>
        <p>But the man at the window kept his back turned, and watched the rain come down.</p>
        <p>"To what end ? To what end ?" be said, tracing an idle pattern on the window-grime. " And yet they say that it is life we get in Lon- don here. Life ? If only they knew, those fools who come in patent-leather and watch us at our gambols !"</p>
        <p>There was scarcely a trace of bitterness in his tone. That had vanished long ago. There was only a faint kind of surprise and half-pathetic wonder. His voice, a trifle thick just now, was a gentleman's, and not all the bloodshot weari- ness of his eyes, the grim irresolution of his month, could hide the stamp of breed. He turned by-and-bye to the table and poured out a half-tumbler of whiskey ; then lit a pipe, and dropped into a chair beside the hearth. And he looked at the girl's face on the mantel-piece, and shivered.</p>
        <p>" What is the good of it all F" lie cried, after a long silence. lie was talking to the photo- graph, for lack of warmer company. " I think you'll be able to understand me, Dolly. You don't know much of this aspect of life, to be sure —there's no reality, you know, about gardens and woods and hayfields with the moonlight on them—but I think I can make it clear to you. It's so lonely, Dot, so abominably lonely There's Hillyard, now ; we used to be as thick thieves at Oxford—good sort, old Hillyard, but as be finds it too far from Hyde Park, W. to Whitechapel, E. It's the sante with all of them; I never see a friend's face, Dolly, unless I snatch time to hunt them up—and that is not often ; besides, they are dropping away one by one— Julian is off to America, Travers is somewhere in the north—they're all out of reach, some- how."</p>
        <p>He looked up again at the photograph with infinite pathos in his face. ''You won't be hard on me, Dolly ?" he muttered. " Letters don't do the same for one, dear, as the sight ,of some- thing human ; and so—" He glanced over his shoulder with a quick, nervous gesture--" so the drink-fiend comes and whispers in one's ear, Dolly ; and the loneliness seems less, and—there's the hell Heaven pity a patient I've to see to," he broke off, with a sudden change of voice. "A case, sir, in the consulting-room," said his housekeeper from the door. '' Very well. I'll be there in a moment."</p>
        <p>Dick ilanvers took a nip from the bottle on the table—" just to steady himself " and went down with uneven steps to his consulting-room. The case, luckily, proved to be of no consequence, and be dismissed it with a bottle of physic and some sound advice. " Keep off the drink," he said, " that is what is ruining you, my num." " Ruining all of us. I fancy," put in the other, with a shrewd glance at him. " Well ? The better able we are, then, to give advice to others," retorted the doctor. " Just so, just so ; good-night." " Good-night." He returned to the shabby living-room, and walked about for a while; and first Ike would stop in front of the photograph on the mantel, and then his eyes would wander to the whiskey- bottle. half-empty, which mocked him from the table.</p>
        <p>" It's this way," he resumed, after another ' furtive nip, " disease and loneliness are all I have 'to live with here, Dolly, and I can't afford yet to take a holiday—can't afford to get away to the meadows and the hedgerows' and the touch of your hand, and—and all that. They said I was foolish, didn't they, to leave a likely partnership and come down here because it was the hub" of everything ? The hub! Oh, Dolly, Dolly, and what a hub it is when one has got to it!" Another sip, and another slight thickening of the voice. " I'm going to give you your free- dom back, Dolly," he went on, sinking into a chair. " It's no use striving—no use—and the practice will begin to slip away again, just when I've made it what it is. That fellow to-night saw clearly what state I was in, and it doesn't do, lassie, it doesn't do. Suppose you could run up and see me ? I wonder if that would alter things at all, child ; I wonder if the sight of you, and the sound of your voice, would bring the bet- ter self back. Fudge ! I'm maudlin. If the thought of you can't, nothing will. The winter's afternoon had closed in now, and the room was almost in darkness. From the chair came a sound of heavy breathing, and the doctor turned in his chair, and turned again' and final- ly went off into a slumber broken by uneasy mutterings. There was another loud ring at the bell presently, and the housekeeper came up and shook the sleeping man. "A gentleman to see you, sir 1" she shouted in his ear.</p>
        <p>" Oh —eh—show him up—where is he now P" muttered the doctor.</p>
        <p>" I left him downstairs, sir, thinking you might want to be—to be undisturbed just now, sir," with a glance at the dwindling contents of the bottle. " What is his name 7" " Mr. Daunav, be said, "Is it? Send him up by all means.—That's right, Daunay," he broke off, as a broad, well- groomed figure appeared in the doorway. " I thought I hadn't a friend left in the world, and here you come. Sit down, man. Pipe ? I don't run to cigars. There's whiskey there, or you van have tea if you prefer it." Daunay gave a quick look at him, and frowned perceptibly. " Tea; thanks.—Well, Manvers, the world isn't using you very well, by the look of you. Working too hard F" "Drinking and working both, to be frank." Again the other frowned ; his friend of old days was fast losing, it was clear, the shame which lead men to conceal their vices. " Why do it I"' he said briefly. "My dear chap, it's not for choice ! Why not come and see a fellow now and then r Why not give one something else to do, as I was saying to myself just now, limn think about one's loneli- ness, whenever work is over for a hit i"</p>
        <p>" Humph ! Ye,+. it inni,t be frightful down here. We don't think of these little th:nge, somehzw, when life pushes a wedge in between I old friends and onrselves.—W bat about Dolly? Still engaged, I take it ?" Minivers coloured faintly. " Ye-es," he said, " though it's pretty cheap of me to keep it up, Its things are. I'm going to make my confession to her, Denney." " Do, and then chnek that liquid there into the street, and go on your knees to the prettiest little girl in Yorkshire, and tell her you've been a fool ; see ?"</p>
        <p>" I—l could scarcely bring ber to Oda sort of life, could I ?'' p‘aid the other with it climatal wave of the hand. " heckles, I'm too far guile, old man."</p>
        <p>'Never. You don't carry any signs of being too far gone ; you're more of a fool than a knave juat yet, my deur gimp, if you'd only credit it." " What are you no to uuwadays i" tahi the</p>
        <p>other, with manitest desire to chimp the tope. " Board of Agriculture and all that ?' " Yes, I go down into the country and sit on gates, and watch the peaceful son of toil at work. and get statistics. A funny life but an easy one —too easy, I begin to fear." " The country ! You don't know what the word brings back to me ! Smell of the bay, and wild thyme growing on the bare upland fields, and clover-scents abroad with the swallow and the cuckoo and the thrush. Daunay, I daren't snatch even an hour for the country ; it's so hard coming back to—this." "To be sure it is. Why not sell your practice here, and cut the neighbourhood, and get clear away to the North, as you meant to do in Ox- ford days !" .</p>
        <p>"Because I'm working up a decent practice here, if I can stick to it ; and afterwards—if I'm not finished first with drink—l can move further westward with a prospect of a decent income." "Look here, my son, you're harping too much on that drink-side, and I propose to do what you won't—take the bottle yonder and empty its con- tents into the street below. No you don't !" be broke off, wresting the bottle from the other's grip, and emptying it—almost on to the head of a passing charwoman, as it chanced. " That's a waste," murmured the doctor, in between sundry loud, exited and unsavoury cries from the woman down below.</p>
        <p>" Not if it's a saving. I'm no saint, Man- vers, bu' rn drop my own allowance of drink to- morrow it it would do you any good. What say you ? Wt were very close together once, old man, and I would do more for you than you guess." The other's eyes softened. " You're a good chap," he said, " but I'm not going to make you play the scape-goat for me." " Oh, well, there's no particular hardship. Goodness knows why we drop into foolish habits at all, if it comes to that, and we're better with- out them. Shall we make it a bargain, Man. Vera ?"</p>
        <p>" No, I'll sink or swim on my own account, thanks all the same—and I'll start by having a cup of tea with you instead of this other stuff.' Tea had just been put on the table—a rather grimy tea-pot, and a rather grimy tray, and a rather grimy plate of bread-and-butter. Datinay shuddered a little—it was all RO foreign this, to Dannay's old-time niceties of feeling. "So the practice is really moving r' he said by-and-bye. " Yes, it's moving at last. They say I'm clever, and that's a vague word that does more than all the medicines in the pharmacy to set a doctor on his legs." " Well, stick to it. Stick to it, do you hear ? Or I'll go straight up to a certain little village that we know of, and I'll bring a certain little lady back with me to see what you are doing.— There ! Time wags faster than I thought for, and I had forgotten a dinner engagement. Bye-bye, Univers ; I'm coining to see you in it day or two, and thereafter as often es the physician directs. Why, you're looking twiea the man you were when I came in." Manvers watched him go down the street be- low, and told himself again what a good fellow this Dsunay was, and wondered if the street were a little less squalid than it had been. Then he moved irresoluL4y about the room ; and last of ell be went, with zig-zag, shifting steps toward the cupboard. " No, not I!" he cried, taking out a fresh bottle of whiskey, and setting it back again. . . . . .</p>
        <p>And to that .'iesolution he held for—three whole days, until, toward the cad of an afternoon as bleak as that which had brought Daunay to see him, he fell again into the old habit. Nothing mattered somehow, so it seemed, except this cursed, lithe. alluring stuff. Slowly his head fell forward as he sat in the chair beside the hearth ; slowly his breathing thickened : and then he awakened with a start as the sound of a heavy step came up the stair, and the door was pushed roughly open. What, Daunay What on earth—" began the doctor.</p>
        <p>" Accident, said Dummy briefly. " Wes coin- ing to see you, and hansom knocked down a lady not half a mile from your door." He laid his burden on the conch, still keeping his broad person between the patient and the doctor.</p>
        <p>'• I—l thought you would prefer me to bring her here to you," ha went on. " Why, yes, but I should have thought the hospital—what's amiss ? Nothing serious, eh F" said Miniver', thickly. " You had better see," said the other, moving aside.</p>
        <p>Manvers put his hand up to his forehead sud- denly as if he had been struck. Maudlin as lie had been a moment since, he showed little trace of it now. " It's Dolly !" he whispered hoarse- ly. " Yes, it'a Dolly ; see to her, old man. I'll wait down below until you're ready with your verdict, and I'll send the landlady up. " Fool, fool l" cried the doctor, as he bent above the quiet figure. " I've had warnings in plenty—warnings in plenty—and yet I must choose just this day on which to drink. Drink— curse it ! And Dolly needing all the care she well can bays." A rapid examination told him there was mis- chief, but not so much as be had feared. A broken arm, and slight concussion of the brain was all.</p>
        <p>"Thank God, there is nothing worse, though it is bad enough," he murmured. " Now, Dolly, if my hand is not as steady as a rock—l'll— what a brute I've been, Dolly 1" All the while he was busy with her injuries, for the housekeeper had already brought up what was needful, and was standing by in readiness to give what help she could. The broken arm was set before the patient re- covered consciousness ; and a faint light of sur- prise, touolied with something that set lifanver'n pulses beating, came into her eyes as they opened, closed, then looked up into the doctor's face again. "Lie still, child. You are in good bands, and nothing much the matter with you," he whis- pered. " I—Dick—l was crossing—" And then she stopped, unable to maintain the thread. "Yes, yes, you had an accident; and now we are putting you to rights.—Ah, there's pain in the arm, is there F Well, we must be brave about it.'' She opened her eyes again, for she was acute- ly conscious of the pain. "Dick, where am I ?" she murmured. "At my house, dear, with one of my band- ages on your arm, and a motherly body here to look well after you. We shall have you about again in no time." The doctor went out presently for something lie needed from the surgery, and found Denney pacing up and down the floor. " Well P" he said.</p>
        <p>"Bad, "bui--nO-Cfs-nger, if all goes well. Denney, you were a brute to knock her down—but what a decent chap you were to bring her here." "I've been thinking," said the other—though he did not add that, amongst other matters, he had been thinking of his own old-time and hope- less passion for Dolly. "She will be better here, I take it 2"</p>
        <p>_ . "Yes, better not move her yet awhile ; the Auk has tried her."</p>
        <p>" Then can I be of nee? I've got a sister— philanthropic, you know, and all that —just her style of thing to come down here, and nurse, and play propriety. Eh ? What do you say ?" " But would she—it seems so tnneli to amlr—"</p>
        <p>"Oh, blem you, ehe'll p.erbu.,de her husband to It, and be's the only obstacle. First•rate, she is lit nursing cages, and she'll be three times a mother to Dolly when once she has seen her face." _</p>
        <p>So it was arranged ; and Dick Manverg, what with work on one hand, and the need of playing host on the other, had little time during the next week to think of his old enemy. Twice the temptation came to him, and twice he went to the door of the patient's room, and knocked and found a refuge. Not that Dolly had submitted without protest to_the..arranizement so quietly planned for her. CATARRHAL BRONCHITIS.</p>
        <p>NEARLY CHOKED. SUFFERED YEARS. CURED BY VEND'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE.</p>
        <p>Mrs. E. WOOD, 73, Bold Street, Northwood, Hanley, Sta ffs, writes, Jane 3rd, 1905 :—" consumed six bottles of VENO'S LIGHTNING Cororr CURE, and can truthfully testify to the marvel lons cure of my case. The doctors pronounced it catarrhal bronchitis ; I was confined to bed, and thought every minute I would choke with the accumulation of phlegm in the head and throat. I could not breathe through my nose ; I was gradually getting worse. The doctors did me no good. I had frequently read of your VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE, and was advised to try it; every bottle made an improvement. Considering that I had been bad for years, off and on, the cure was a miracle. Ask for VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGLI CURE at Chemists, L3d., is. VA., and 2s. 9d. Sold everywhere</p>
        <p>At was aosnra, it was ainicuit, it was everytblng in the world that she should be here—here in Dick's own house. But Dolly was a prisoner, perforce; and the kindly nurse who had come to tend her would listen to no distressing talk of any sort; and Dick himself kept quietly in the back- ground as far as possible until a certain afternoon of late December.</p>
        <p>"Dolly, I've come for my pardon," he said abruptly, kneeling by her couch. "Your—your pardon? For what, Dick ?" " Didn't you get my last letter, then ?" "Yes, it was sent after me to London, and I was puzzled—and I believe I was thinking over it when the hansom knocked me down. That was why I didn't want to stay here—you seemed so strange in your letter, and so cold." "And well I might. I wanted you to give me up, Dolly—yes, I did I To give me up." "To—give--you—up ?" she echoed. "Let me get rid of my confession, Dolly. I can't rest until I have."</p>
        <p>"Yes," she said in a colourless voice. "You'll not care afterwards, perhaps, but I must tell you. You see, Dolly, it's—it's a bit lonely down /ere ; and the loneliness ate into me, somehow, and temptation came ; and I was a weak fool, dear." " flow? Temptation of what kind 7" she flashed, with sudden interest. " It was—it was—drink." "Ah !" with a sigh almost of relief. " You can go on. Dick, now." . . .</p>
        <p>„ He told her t,hen the whole way of it: and she listened till be had done ; and her face would have been altogether grave but for a certain something in the clear-hazel eyes which Manvers might have read had he had wit enough! ” Your letter was cruel, do you know, Dick ? 0.. J wish you had told me all, instead of letting me think what I would. I was in town, too, and was Planning a surprise visit to you just when the leiter came—and London seems emptier than any place on earth, Dick, when one is wretched. You can guess what I said to myself, when I received a contrite note from you, full of vague allusions. " Why, yen !" he cried. " Dolly, Dolly, how could Ibe so blind ? But how could you be so blind as to think there was ever another woman made but you !" "There are hundreds, and I—Dick, you must leave me alone for awhile," she broke off. " You must let me think over and over everything, for lam all bewildered. And—will you promise— will you promise—" He understood her, and his humiliation, deep before, grew still more poignant. He pictured the sodden days, the restless nights, that had gone to make him what he was when Dolly first was brought here in need of all his skill. Suppose be had bungled the setting of the arm, had had to break it again afterwards, and re-set it, and give for his only excuse the fact that drink had mas- tered him ? Suppose he had bungled more than this—bungled his whole life ? " Dolly," he said, "the thirst will come again, and I shall fight it. And when I've fought it till it comes no more, shall I be free to—" "My dear," she interrupted softly, " have I Hot watched your face through these last weeks? Do I not know what it has meant to you? And you—you may take forgiveness now, if you've the need for it."</p>
        <p>Dick had been looking out into the street and down the squalid length of it, as he turned to meet the other's eyes, a shaft of sunlight passed, lighting the scene into something gracioms, benign almost. And Whiteohapel was full of the scent of hay fields and of hedgerows crammed with wild rose blossom and with hawthorn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS. NON-TRADERS' FIGHT.</head>
        <p>MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS. NON-TRADERS' FIGHT.</p>
        <p>The Conduct of the Town's Affairs.</p>
        <p>"THE COUNCIL RULED BY OFFICIALS." The Charges Against Councillors. (contiaum on Page 5)</p>
        <p>A meeting of Non-Traders was held in the Co-opera- tive Hall, S inkey-street, on Monday evening,in support of the canditattire of Councillor Benson in the 'rown Hall Ward. Owing to somewhat short notice the meeting was only poorly attended. Mr. James Dodds presided and was supported by Mr. Robert Cameron, M.P., Councillors Pemberton, Benson and Skellaiad, Messrs. H. Thornton, S. Kenyon, and Cann. The CHAIRMAN said that he had given expression in the papers of Saturday to his opinion with regard to later development in the ward and especially with regard to the action of one of the two great politicll parties in deciding to ignore the work which the Non- trading party had done in Warrington dining the past four years. He was a Liberal but he was first of all for clean and honest government and he would not be con- nected with any party which would not put that in the forefront of their programme. In Warrington they would have to fight to bring the Liberal party back to the principles on its banner—retrenchment and reform —and above that to the principle of purity in election and in Government. (Applause.) Mr. KENYON moved and Mr. THORNTON seconded a resolution in support of the candidature of Mr. Benson. Councillor PEMBERTON supported the resolution. In Warrington, he said, the officials of the Corporation were the masters instead of the members of the Cor- poration, and until they had men in the Council who would devote their time to the work, and who would insist upon being masters they would not have pros- perity, ,r a proper return frotn the undertakings carried on by the Corporation. The Corporation investmeMs amounted to £1,000,000, and if they only returned a reasonable percentage they would have no rates to pay at all. If they were not satisfied with the enormous expenditure which had been going on they must put their foot down very firmly. (Hear, hear.) A contract had recently been let for the erection of a new school for £13,000, but the total cost of the school, before it was ready for the scholars and the furniture and fittings were complete, would be £25,000. He would like to refer to another question. There was a certain gentle- man who was not a representative of the ratepayers, havinm•retired from the Council, who was at the Town .Hall ten times oftener than he (Councillor Pemberton) was and there mostly in company with the Town Clerk. That gentleman, who should know very little of the business of the CoToration, knew more about it than any Non-Tr ,der who was a member of the Town Council. There was another matter to which be would like to refer' which was Councillor Benson' d position with regard to the making of a new street from Sankey-stru at to Bylands-street. The Non-Traders opposed the pro- position tooth and nail but it was adopted. Councillor Benson's shop alone stood in the way of the completion of the work. Mr. Benson did not want to sell his shop and would be perfectly satisfied if the Corporation would leave him in peace and quietness. Mr. Benson said "I do'nt want to sell my shop, but if you take it you must give me a good position somewhere else." That was a perfectly proper and just demand. (Applause). He understood the other side were going to try and make something out ot that. If that was so all they could say was that they were hard up. (Applause). He would like to know bow many gentlemen there were present who would go to the Council Chamber and stand the insults and slurs of the present Mayor. There were some resolutions on the agenda paper for the not meet- ing of the Council, which he hoped they would read, with regard to the Mayor for his services. What he could not stand was that gentlemen who in an anti-room at the Town Hall spoke against the Mayor would publicly thank him for the courteous and fair manner in which he had carried on the business of the Cornell." (Applause.) The resolution was carried.</p>
        <p>Mr. Robert CAMERON, M.p., expressed the pleasure it afforded him to assist Mr. Dodds in his battle for fair play and purity. The fight in Warrington had been intensely interesting to him because he was somewhat of an experienced hand in such fights. Mr. Cameron referred to .his long connection with various public bodies and said be therefore knew what elections were. He knew how they were won some- times—(laughter)—by one man having a bigger purse than another. He never had a big purse himself —(laughter)—but if he were a millionaire he would not bribe on any consideration. (Anplause.) He admired democracy too much to allow it to be spoiled as an instrument for doing the highest and best work for this or any other country. The moment democracy became corrupt, democracy was no better than Czarism or Laperialisru or anything else. The nen in democracy must be true and above being bribed. Was not that the spirit they were want- ing to diffuse ; was it not the condition of affairs, as far as he heard, in that Borough ?—that they wanted purity, so that no man would come and ask for their votes because he was rich or because he would do them some favour in return, but simply because the wanted to do good, efficient,necessary public work. It was all im- portant that there should be no bribery and that public work should be done in day light before the world. He hoped the penalty for bribery would be increased both in regard to locil authorities sad the House of Commons. Mr. Cameron proceeded to argue in favour of full publicity in the work of Town Councils and emphased the increasing importance of the position of a Councillor in view of the passing of the Education Act. When Councillor Pemberton was delivering his vigorous speech, he (Mr. Cameron) could not help ask- ing himself "Is that gentleuMn actually a Tory ?" (Laughter.) It was inconceivable. He was about the strongest Radical he (Mr. Cameron) ever catne across (Laughter.) Any man who denounced corruption and who was for making thins straight and right was, in his opinion a true Liberal.ll Tories were going to work in that spirit they would have to call the Tories Liberals and Liberals Tories. (Laughter.) He hoped they would</p>
        <p>keep up the standard of purity and that they would unsparingly denounce any selfishness or underhand work that they could not approve. (Applause). Mr. NORBURY WILLIAMS said one of the difficulties the Non-Tralers had not yet overcome was that their party in the Town Council was not yet a majority and therefore worked under con,iderable difficulties. He had been blamed for criticising the Mayor. In England we all liked to speak well of dignities and dignitaries, and to obey those who were set over us. But he wanted to say what he had said before, that there had been shown by the Mayor in regard to the members of the Non-Trading party who happened to be in a minority such partiality as against them that it called for the reprobation of every burgess who believed in fair play. (Applause.) He wanted to express the strong hope that when the Mayor took his position for the third year he would accord more fair treatment than he (Mr. Williams) believed he had done to the gentlemen who represented the Non-Trading movement which he (Mr. Williams) believed had the great majority of the people and the best people behind it. (Applause). Three years ago Liberals, Good Teruplars and Conservatives formed an unholy and outrageous combination to keep Councillor Pemberton out of the council. He believed that alliance put back the temperance movement in Warring- ton by years and years. Now, however, when these men again had the chance to oppose Councillor Pemberton they slunk into their tents. They were ashamed of the combination they formed before, for it had done infinite harm to the good men who allowed themselves to got entangled in it. Very shortly, continued Mr. Williams, he was told they were going to have one or two very serious law actions. All sorts of threats were made as to certian people who were to become the implicated and the serious consequences that were going to arise. The names of the two men against whom proceedings were to be taken had been kept a profound secret. No one knew them, he was told. (Laughter.) But with regard to one of them he thought the following lines which he read some years ago would apply .</p>
        <p>They q!ggec! a pit,</p>
        <p>They died it de.ep, They meant it for their brothers ; But in the end They all fell in The trap they'd laid for others. (Laughter). They were going to see some stirring things, he expected, with reference to those actions. So far as they were aware at the present moment the trading against which the Non-Traders had fought was abolished, but he contended the change would never have been made if it had depended on a great many men who were in the Council. Mr. Williams endorsed the contention of Mr. Dodds that municipal elections should not be fought by political p trties merely to keep their machinery in order to test the feeling of the electorate as to how they would fare in the Parlia- mentary contests. (Applause.) On the motion of Councillor SKELLAND seconded by „.. S.KENYON a vote of thanks was accorded to the Chairman and speakers.</p>
      </div>
