      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>MR. DODDS AND TFIE GIMBAL PARTY.</head>
        <p>MR. DODDS AND TFIE GIMBAL PARTY.</p>
        <p>A meeting was held in the Co-operative Hall, Winmck-street, on Tuesday evening in support of the candidature of Mr Dickenson in the Bewsey Ward. Mr. DODDS, who presided over a good attendance, said it had been proved over and over again that the introduction of party politics tato municipal matters had almost invariably resulted in extravagance and corruption Did they wonder then that those of them who had borne the heat and burden of the struggle for civic purity were highly incensed when a few weeks ago it was publicly stated that one of the two parties had decided to promote contest in every ward this year, not to improve the character of the representation, but to get their fighting machinery into working order—in other words to prostitute the best local interests of the town treat them as mere counters in the parliamentary game? Did they wonder that he had felt it his duty to publicly protest against the action of the Liberal Executive. (Applause). During the whole period of their agitation the pullers of the strings of the Liberal Party, not the rank and file had done all they could to defeat them. Ile would not forget this, nor would many hundreds of other sound Liberals. Mr. A. EIULME moved a resolution in support of the candidature of Mr. Dickenson. Councillor PEMBERTOIi seconded. He had that night, he said, made some remarks in the Council Chamber, which if anyone had told him he would have made he would have bet 100 to 1 he would not have done. In this country everyone was supposed to have justice but he had begun to realize the farce of it all and to realize the difficult position in which the workingmen of the country were placed and until they recognized that they must work together for one common end their position would never bring about any improvement. In this country they conformed with certain rules, but when the vote of thanks to the Mayor was proposed in the Council Chamber he objected to it. (Applause.) Ile did not see how any right-minded man could pass such a resolution as was submitted. Had it not been proposed that they should thank the Mayor for his unvarying courtesy and impartiality " he would not have opposed it. (Applause.) The resolution was carried. Mr. DODDS said he had been handed a circular issued by Mr. P. Peacock in which he said " yet this immacu- late individual stoops so low as to sign this despicable circular purely and simply to vent his private feelings. If Mr. Doods has any objection to my candidature let him come forward in an honest stri,glaforward manner, and not descend to such mean subterfuges." During the whole of the agitation, continued Mr. Dodds, he had never issued a letter or circular to which he had not attached his name. In the circular referred to by Mr. Peacock his name was at the bottom. Where was the mean subterfuge about that? (Applause.) He I was not seeking any popularity in lVarrington. There was not a public office that he would accept, but he wanted to raise the tone of public life. Applause). Mr. Itouxra at-MI:RON, ALP., briefly addressed the meeting, urging the necessity of the working men voting for men who would watch their interests, and who would work for the health and education of the people. (Applause). A vote of thanks to the Chairman and Mr. Cameron concluded the meeting.</p>
      </div>
