      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>DEATH OF A WIDNES EN7ATAVOR.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF A WIDNES EN7ATAVOR.</p>
        <p>The death took place on Sunday night, at Widnes, of Mr. Samuel Sadler, a forniqr Mayor of Widnes. For some years deceased had been residing at Blackpool, where he kept a boarding house. On Wednesday last he came to Widnes, and took up his residence ln Witt road. He had been in a ael:cat6 state of health for some time but nevertheless his death came very unexpectedly. He was a member of the old local board at the incorpora- tion of the boroagh. In the year 1892 he was elected to the aldermanic bench, after which, in 1894, he was unanimously chosen Mayor, in succession to Mr F, H. Gossage, the first Mayor of Widnes. He retained office for two years. On November 9th, 1901, he severed his connection with the Council. Deceased was about sixty-four years of age.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS. ' The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guardians was held at Dutton on Tuesday when there were preset :—Messrs. A. R. Norman (presiding), C. Roylance, J. Bolshaw, H. Eyes, P. Monnileld, G. Eger- ton Warburton, J. Stokes, J. Whitlow, A. Broome, 'l'. Baxter, H. M. Davies, J. Smith, T. Wright, S. Wood- , ward, W. Harrison, G. Pilling, S. Horton, J. L. Jones, E. llretherton, R. A. Chrimes, J. Fryer, J. Spruce, W. M. Morris, P. Coll ins, J. Douglas, J. Whitley, T. Antwis, i Admiral Jones-Parry, and Messrs. G. F. Ashton (clerk), I W. E. Hough (assistant clerk), \V. De Prez, F. Pearson, I and 1. Cook (relieving officers), and J. Cunnah (Work- house master).—Mr. Woodward reported payment of £l2B in out-relief. The Board had a balance in hand of £2,791 Os. ld.—Mr. Spruce reported that the number of inmates in the house was 131, as against 138 for the cor- responding period of last year. All the beds except one on the male side were occupied.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>ASTHMA CAIT BE CUBED.</head>
        <p>ASTHMA CAIT BE CUBED.</p>
        <p>A Noted Physician Will Prove this to all Sufferers</p>
        <p>The astonishing statement that Asthma can be cured, coming from so well-known authority as Dr. Rudolph Schiffrnan, will be of interest to asthma sufferers. The experience of most Asthmatics has been that little if any relief has been afforded them by the methods heretofore employed, and, in fact, the disease has up to now been regarded as incurable. This noted physician has, however, after a lifelong study of Asthma and kindred diseases discovered a remedy which not only instantly relieves the severest cases of Asthma and Bronchitis, but has actually cured thousands whose cases had been pro- nounced incurable. So complete is Dr. Schiffmands conndence in his remedy that he requests this paper to announce that he offers to send a liberal sample pack- age of" Schiffmann's Asthma Cure " free of charge to all persons sending him their name and address, plainly written on a post-card, within the next 4 days. He believes that an actual test will be the most con- vincing, and in fact the only way to overcome the natural prejudice of thousands of Asthmatics who have heretofore sought relief in vain. Although most chemists in Great Britain have sold his Asthma Care ever since it was introduced to the British public, still the Doctor fears there are some persons who have never heard of it, and it is with a view to reaching these that he makes this liberal offer. An opportunity to test, without cost, a remedy se celebrated and promising so much certainly should be eagerly grasped by every sufferer. Address Dr. R. Schillman, British Depot, 32, Snow Hill, London, E.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>Ambulance Competition at Warrington.</head>
        <p>Ambulance Competition at Warrington.</p>
        <p>The annual competition for the Crosfield Challenge Shield was held at Warrington on Saturday, in :Ile parade room of the Police buildings The competing teams were: Winsford, Caine, Crompton, Liverpool and Bootle, and _after a close contest Crompton were awarded the shield. A handsome present, consisting of several of 'Messrs. Crosfield's specialities tastefully arranged in an ornamental box, was given to each competitor of the winning team. After the contest au excellent hot-put supper was served at the Patten Anus Hotel. Mr. Studer, acting superintendent of the Cros- field division, took thv chair, and proposed the toast of "The King." Colonel Trimble who judged the cop).- peting teams gave the health of" The Firm," with which he coupled the name of the Chairman. The encouragement which 'had been given to ambulanco work, through the generosity of the firm in providing a challenge shield was, he said, very considerable, and had been especially helpful in stimulating interest, in the work and in causing the various corps and divisions to. endeavour to secure the best possible results. Hearty cheers were, given for Messrs. Crostield and Sons, Ltd., aiso for Pr. Markel, Colonel Trimble and the Chairman Mr. (;-. Dale responded on behalf of the firm. The interest which Messrs. Crostield had taken in ambulance woik for many years showed no sign of abatement. On the other hand there was evidence of increasing interest. The recent formation of the Crosfield Division of the St. John Ambulance Brigade in itself testified to the fact of the existence of a desire to give increased support and encouragement to a work Wiiich was obviously so commendable and praiseworthy. (Applause). He hoped that at their next Shield Competition they might have with t'leni 3 greater number of competing squids. (Hear, hear), Mr. Studer also replied. They would iemember that last year, on a similar occasion, he promised that in l% months a squad of Crosfield's ambulance division would take part in the competition for the Shield. As they , knew, the promise had not been fulfilled. The reason was that after further consideration it was considered more fitting, that a team representing the donors of the shield should refrain from competing. (blear, hearJ He trusted that the teams which had not been succesr fill that day would do their utmost to increase their present efficiency and that the winners on the other hand, would not rest upon their laurels but endearotir to held the trophy for a longer period than l months. (Applause.) During the evening songs and recitations I were rendered by members of the ambulance division-</p>
        <p>Wycliffe Young People's Society. A highly successful meeting of this Society was Lela on Monday evening, Mr. J. Gauld presiding .ever very large attendance. Mr. James Ball read an able paper entitled " A peep at I.4.mglelluw." He briefly reviewed the poet's life and read several of his poeult pointing out that the simplicity of the works jo particular commended to the reader. A dis- cussion followed the reading of the paper. During the evening several of lAmgfollow's be4r* known works which had been set to music were sung and were highly appreciated. Mr. Frank Brown effectively sang 'r The Village Blacksmith," and thl voices of Mr. A. Griffiths and Mr. W. Barnes blende" well in " Excelsior." A glee " The sea hath pearls " was interpreted in meritorious style 11 Misses lEbbert, Annie Wallington, Atkinsw.i. a0(1 Beswick, and -Messrs. J. Gauld, W. Brown, J. Atkin- son aud W. Barnes.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Watts and Co., are issuing for the Rationalist Press Association two " sixpennies " of a constructive and ethical character. The first is a revise.' edition or Mr. Edward Clodd's "Jesus of Nazareth," being " appreciation of the founder of the Chilstian faith and or his significance in the world's,history, as viewed with reference to the hest, established conclusions of the higher criticism. The second is an exposition of Ethicai Religion, by Mr. W. M. Salter, a well-known Anierie4l3 author, who writes in a catholic and tolerant spirit. The same firm are publishing, also at sixpence, Stewart L. Nlurray's The Future Peace or the Anglo- Saxons, with an impressive introduction by Earl Roberts ; and The Agnostic Annual for 1906. containing articles by Mr. G. M. Trevelyan, Mr. J. M. Robertson,Professo! W. H. Hudson, Mr. Joseph McCabe, and other we,111,:. known liberal thinkers, and a poem by Mr. Eden Ptu potts. Among Messrs. Watt's other new publicatic!nl are :—Did Shakespeare write " Titus Andronicus ? by Mr. J. M. Roberton ; the Religion that Fulfils,,3 simple account of Posisivism, with notes and comment°, ' by Mr. F. J. Gould ; and a Plea for Pessimism, by Mr; H. D. Irvine, being an introduction to the study 0' Schopenhauer.</p>
      </div>
