      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>Mr DEAR CHILD.</head>
        <p>Mr DEAR CHILD.</p>
        <p>I have an announcement to. make this week which I am sure will interest all my young friends. I have arranged with Messrs. Hovis, Limited, of Macclesfield, to send one of their latest puzzle novelties post free to every reader of the "Children's Corner," The puzzle is very ingenious, and it interested me for quite a long time. I have no doubt but what a great number of you will solve it if you try. Messrs. Hovis have kindly offered to pre- sent a handsome blotter bound in cloth, with gold lettering, to all my little friends who send correct solutions. Be sure to send a post-card or letter to-day to Messrs. Hovis, Limited,,Mac- clestield, saying that you are a reader of the "Children's Corner," and would like one of their puzzles. When you hare solved the puzzle, return it to Uncle Ned, office of this paper. . . .</p>
        <p>-- I have received so many letters this week and solutions to puzzles that it would be quite impossible to acknowledge all, so I will only publish one letter. I have awarded the prize for correct answer to puzzle to Albert Brooks. Many of you were correct, but I have to award the prize to the first answers that I receive. I will give another box of Fry's chocolates to the first sending in correct solutions to the puzzles given below. With all good wishes, from</p>
        <p>UNCLE NED.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—l should like to become a member of "The Children's Corner League." I have answered the puzzles this week, and I hope they are right. My auntie has a Chinese dog, his name is "Chang," his tongue is nearly black. You have got quite a lot of members now. Have you many in I Here are the answers to the puzzles. Now I must close, with best wishes.—l remain your loving niece. C. B.</p>
        <p>A HANDSOME PRESENT FOR READERS OF THE "CHILDREN'S CORNER."</p>
        <p>If my young friends will kindly refer to the letter at the top of this column, they will find particulars of an ingenious puzzle novelty offered free to all applying for same to Messry Hovis Bread Company, Macclesfield. If you can solve the puzzle, send it addressed to Uncle Ned, care of the office of this paper, and a handse-,e blotter will be sent post free. I hope you will all take advantage of this free offer, and send a postcard at once for the puzzle.</p>
        <p>LIST OF NEW MEMBERS.</p>
        <p>Betty Hargreaves. Lily Walmsley. Alice Ireland. Willie Haworth. Jack Ingham. Albert Tookey. Herbert Cooper. John Schofield. Steven Maciutyre. Harry Hargreaves. Nellie Ireland. Jack Holgate. Marjorie Owen. Cissie Bates. William Clifton.</p>
        <p>twit- i did ecause it</p>
        <p>was night and they were asleep. A toad hopped up the porch steps, and looked at the box. His eyes shone like little stars.</p>
        <p>"What's in he asked. • "Fireworks," answered some small, crackling voices, through a wide crack in the top. "Oh I I see," said the toad. "What a fib I You don't," said the voices. "Well, I know," said the toad. "What do you know?" asked the queer voices.</p>
        <p>"I know what you've come here for," answered the toad. "You've come here to go off. You'll go off to-morrow night. I saw a lot of your relatives last Guy Fawkes' day. Fine fellows they were, but too bright to last. And such a fuss and a noise as they made when they did go off! Fizz-izz-izz-izz-k-r-k-k-r-k-k-r-k- -k-r-k splutter-splutter-splutter swish-ish-ish- ish—bangl—bangl—bang—" But, before he could say another word, " Good-night I " said the small voices, in banes more crackling than ever.</p>
        <p>"What " asked the toad. " Good-night I 'F snapped the voices. "Oh! good-night," said the toad; and he turned around and hopped down the steps.</p>
        <p>[To ,be continued.]</p>
        <p>A MUTUAL MISTAKE.</p>
        <p>'Two ladies stood on the doorstep of a friend's house waiting for admission, and they became very impatient at the delay. "It's very odd to be kept waiting at Mrs. Darley's," said one. " The door is usually opened so promptly." "So it is. I'm getting very tired." I wonder if there is absolutely no one in the house?"</p>
        <p>"Of course there are people in. We'll ring again." "You rang before, didn't you?" "Why no. I thought you rang !" "Well, I was sure you rang. How ridiculous!" "Yes, isn't it?" Then one of them rang, and the door was opened.</p>
