      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, November 4,</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, November 4,</p>
        <p>[ALL RIGHTS RESERVED.] TRAVEL EXTRAORDINARY,</p>
        <p>EUSTACE REYNOLDS-BALL (Author of" Row to Visit the Mediterranean." 4.c.).</p>
        <p>It would be interesting, if not altogether pro- fitable, to attempt to forecast the future of rapid transit as applied to passenger traffic. Speculative writers like Jules Verne and H. G. Wells, whose aim is to popularise science for the general reader, haVe indulged in various dreams of the ultimate goal of the development of locomotion. Whether the problem of rapid transit for the public will be solved by aerostats or aeroplanes—anylice air ships—which are at present little more than costly toys, or whethex the solution will be found—at all events in the great cities-..in the development of pnetunatic tubes, who can say?</p>
        <p>As regards private as opposed to public transit, everything seems to point to the almost universal use of the Rotor Car—at present the recreation of the rich. Like the bicycle it will no longer be a luxury or a recreation but an article of general utility and convenience. The development of aerial navigation seem at present temporarily arrested, indeed the dependence of these air ships on favourable climatic conditions is likely to prevent any rapid development of 'this dangerous, as well as costly, method of progression. Pneumatic railways seem to offer a more reasonable probability of success. The pneumatic principle has been applied for the transmission of letters and small parcels in London, Paris, Vienna, and Berlin for many years. In London indeed, the officials of the General Post. Office had long employed pneumatic tubes for the transport of packages before the French postal authorities had thought of 'utilising the pneumatic principle. But the original system- was, however, only given a short trial hi Loudon. The sensational story of the discovery of a lost pneuniptic tunnel in London some two or three years ago, proved, it will be remembered, a veritable gold mine to the Tit-Bitian jonnulls of London. But as a matter of fact, this kind of railway for passenger traffic is no new thing. In 1865 a pneumatic railway line for passengers was constructed in the Crystal Palace Grounds. It consisted of a single line of rails laid in a tunnel 600 yards long. A single carriage was shot through the tube 1 ke a catapult by the agency of compressed air, while any dangerous impact at the terminus of the tunnel was minimised by the use of very strong buffers. The public not yet habituated to " looping the loop," " shooting the chute " and other violent delights, failed to patronise this novel railway, and it was in operation for it short period only.</p>
        <p>RAILWAYS.</p>
        <p>.Railway travel, if only ordinary services are included, not counting those performances. which are effected under peculiar and specially favourable conditions, such as the famous runs of electric motors on the Elbertield Electric Railway, Great Britain certainly holds her own. The best British long distance record which is also a " non-stop" one, is that of the G.W.R. from Paddington to Plymouth with their Cornish express, which leaves Paddington daily at 10.0 a.m., performing the 246 miles to Plymouth with- out n break in three hours and forty-five minutes. But for long distances runs this is altogether eclipsed . by the extraordinary journey of a L. and N. :W. express from London to Aberdeen as long ago as 1895, the distance (540 miles) was clone in 512 ininnees, which is certainly not only an English, but a world's record. . .</p>
        <p>, The fastest authentic record of locomotive engine speed, irrespective of distance, is also held by the G. W. H. An engine once on a trial speed trip did a quarter of a mile with a flying start in 9. 2. seconds, which is at the rate of some 98 miles an hour.</p>
        <p>Another note worthy performance of the kind is that of the L. and :N. W. with a non-stop express run from Wigan to Willesden a dis- tance of 188 miles.</p>
        <p>Some very creditable runs without a stoppage are performed in the trains-de-luxe services of the Continent. The best of these, now that thg, speed of the famous " Sud Express " has beef?' reduced, is that of the Rome Express from Paris, the Dijon-Culoz section (152 miles), being run without a stoppage. Next in order of merit is the Boulogne run of the London-Paris Express (leaving Char- ing Ctoss at 2.20 p.m.), the journey being iwoeinplisligd in the oci boars. .</p>
        <p>Another long distance non-stop run is that of the P. &amp; 0. Brindisi Express in the section from Bologna to Ancona (126 miles) but the time is not remarkable. 3 hours and a quarter being consumed. But the whole journey Prom London to Brindisi (1460 miles in 441. hours) is still, I believe the fastest run of that distance in Europe. • In America the longest and quickest run is that of the ifiunous • " Twentieth Century Ex- press," which runs from New York to Chicago (621 miles). in 12 hours 42 minutes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>STEAM-SRIPS.</head>
        <p>STEAM-SRIPS.</p>
        <p>Turbine engined steamers easily hold the speed record. The greatest speed known was that once attained by the ill-fated Viper which ou one occasion steamed one mile at the rate of 41 miles an hour. The World's record for ocean liners is held by Germany with the Ham- burg-American Company's Liner, the " Deutsch- land " with a speed of 23i knots. It is true that the four Irish Mail Steamers running from Holyhead can boast of the same speed, and it is believed that . the London and Brighton's new turbine steamer " Queen " is equal to 24 knots an hour, but these short trip steamers should of •conrse, be placed in a different category. The maritime sprints of these ferry- boats can scarcely be compared with the Ocean voyage of over 3000 miles from Cherbourg to New York, in which the extraordinary average of 23 knots per hour has been maintained through- out by the Deutschland. Of English Steamers the . Cunard's crack, " Lucania " comes a good second with an average of nearly 22 knots from Queenstown to New York. Of Eastern records, the Caledonia, the crack boat of the P. and 0. Company, has steamed from Plymouth to Boni- , bay in seventeen days, a wonderful feat when the necessity of slowing down in the Suez Canal is considered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>MOTOR CARS.</head>
        <p>MOTOR CARS.</p>
        <p>All world records were broken in 1004 in a most sensational manner in Florida. This is mainly owing to the accidental dis- covery that Ormond Beach in Florida, offers at once the speediest and safest racing track in the world—the surface being better adapted for speed than any known artificial course. On this remarkable track Mr. W. K. Vanderbilt performed the following unique feats, a mile with flying start in 35 seconds i.e. at the rate of 102 miles an hoer ; ten miles in 6 minutes 50 seconds (83 miles an hour) ; and 50 miles in 40m1nutes 49 seconds (71 miles an hour).</p>
        <p>For a series of races in which a score of cars can race aide by side this course is unparalleled, and oartfrinly from the spectator's poixt of view a race against the 'stop-watch merely is compara- tively tame. The Ornmnd beach is for many miles level as a billiard table, and possessed of a hard and elastic surface, over which cars can travel with less loss of power than over the best macadamised or " Westrumite " road. This sand course proved even a faster route than the frozen surface of Lake St. Clair, fm which, earlier in the year, Mr. Harry Ford had done the mile in 39 2-5 sec., or at the speed of 91 miles an hour. He was the first motorist to exceed 90 wiles an hour, mid some doubts were cast on the genuineness of the record until a fortnight later the record. was beaten. several times on the Florida coast. _ .</p>
        <p>It is said that the effect on a spectator watching a car approaching at the rate of 90 or 100 miles am hour is at first it little upsetting to tia• niTves. A black mass seems to shoot trout the arol,lld. and almost before it can be</p>
        <p>uniringuisnea it is upon rac pcvlsror, aim In a few more in4onts it has passed beyond the horizon. At. the nuunent mt passing it produces •t il"nn the observer: Indeed there seems actually an element at dan- ger in the extraordinary draught it, produces. These are however American records. In Europe the motor car record with a flying start is held by Rigolly who recently accomplished a wonderful performance at Nice covering the kilometre in 23 3/sth. seconds with a Gobron Brillie hundred horse-power car. This feat is all the more remarkable in as much as it was accomplished not on a prepared track,ibut on the Promenade des Anglais at Nice, by no means an ideal course for racing motors. This„reoord does not seem to have been officially guaranteed. However a performance exceeding this by 3/5 sec. has recently been officially credited to Baron de Caters, at Ostend on May 15th, 1304, who did a Kilometre with a flying start in 23 seconds dead. MOTOR CYCLES.</p>
        <p>In motor-cycling the two best long distance runs on record are Arnott's ride from the Land't End to John o• tirrout.s House. in 2 days, 11 hours and 45 minutes in July 1902, and Bucquet's performance in the Great Paris-Madrid Race who rode from Paris to Bordeaux in three minutes under nine hours. But it is significant that an ordinary bicycle (contemptuously termed "push bicycle" by motorists) has beaten the motor-cycle _in the hour's distance record, T. Hall at Auteuil in 1903 having covered over fifty-four miles in an hour, beating the motor- cycle hour's record by several miles.</p>
        <p>ICE-BOATS.</p>
        <p>Hitherto one of the fastest means of locomotion known was an ice yacht, and on the Hudson it is a commonplace feat for an ice boat with a good wind on the quarter to beat an express travelling at a speed of fifty to sixty-five miles an hour,—the Central railway for many miles running along the shore of the Hudson. There exist indeed many "newspaper records" of 85 miles an hour, but these are not Droperly authenticated.</p>
      </div>
