      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>urrEßs TO THE EDITOR</head>
        <p>urrEßs TO THE EDITOR</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS.</p>
        <p>Authors of letters intended for publication must in an cases send their names and addresses to the Editor as a guarantee of good faith.</p>
        <p>National Service. sir,—l admire your impartiality in inserting letters written against your views more than I admire the logic contained in those views. You will have to find -stronger arguments than those you have yet used if you i wish to stem the tide which s gradually swelling in favour of the National Service League proposals. There are, I believe, some 17,000 Quakers in this -country, and if they hold that it is against their religion to help to defend their fellow citizens or to protect their homes from insult, an exception would be made in their case. But it is questionable whether such would be the view of most Quakers. Certainly neither George Fox nor Isaac Pennington were opposed to the use of force in the cause of justice as in the case of 'people defending themselves against foreign invasion.' You repeat that what is required is more encourage- ment for the volunteers and that this will solve the military problem. Of course, if you put on one side as of no value the opinion of Lord Roberts and of every other soldier of authority, that the present volunteer training is totally inadequate, it is futile to continue the argument. The minimum amount of training that will suffice to enable a citizen army to withstand thoroughly drilled and disciplined troops, is that approximating to the training of the Swiss Militia certainly not less than two or three months consecutive training. Here then is the point: how are you going to gut the volunteers to put in this training unless you make a national affair of the whole business? unless l each man does his share, employer and employs alike?</p>
        <p>If what we are urging was going to be a bad thing for the working class Icould better understand you, as representing the Liberal Party, taking the line you have done. But as the proposed training affords the work- man a holiday which it would be impossible for him to take in any other way, it is difficult to see why the Liberal party, which professes to be the friend of the workingman, should thus stand in his light. National training would do more than any other measure towards abolishing the appalling misery of our large towns for it would take the first step necessary, viz., focus public attention on the evils of slum existence.-4 am yous</p>
        <p>GEORGE CROSFIELD.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>Social Service League.</head>
        <p>The Social Service League.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Below, I send you a copy of a resolution which was passed unanimously at a well-attended meeting of the Committee of the Social Service League held on Thursday evening last. Resolved : That the Committee of the Social Service League, having gone into the whole circumstances of the recent Lecture delivered by Mrs. Cranston, recog- nises and endorses the action of the President, believing that he acted in the best interests of the League ; and also thanks Messrs. J. Crosfleld and Sons, Ltd., for placing their Assembly Room at the disposal of the Lecturer. That a copy of this resolution be sent to the local newspapers. Thanking you, I am yours &amp;c., EDMUND OSBORNE. (Financial Secretary Social Service League.)</p>
        <p>--- Sir,—ln reply to Dr. Markel's letter in the "Examiner" of Saturday last, I can assure him that there was not the slightest intention on my part of casting any reflection either on himself or his company. There was the fact that Mrs. Cranston's lecture was publicly reported to be under the auspices of the social Service League. This was wrong, and I wrote to correct the error. The lecture not t eing an S.S.L. undertaking, I could scarcely feel slighted at not being communicated with. It now appears from the letter of Mr. Dunnico that they (to use his own words) "studiously steered clear of stating in any shape or form that the lecture was under the auspices of the League"; yet in spite of the care they took to avoid dhe error the lecture did actually come to be erroneously reported as an S.S.L. undertaking, It must, one would think, be inexplicable to Dr. Markel that I should be met with such a torrent of abuse from the very two gentle- men who had done their best to avoid its being linked to the S.S.L.</p>
        <p>The explanation seems to be that while they " technically" kept clear the lecture was advertised in such a way as to lead the public to suppose it to be under the auspices of our League. The circular was headed "Social Service Lecture," the advertisement was headed "Warrington District Social Service League," and yet Mr. Dunnico has the impudence to charge me with quibbling. Further, Mr. Dunnico did, as he acknowledges, say that it was thought he should take the chair because he happened to be the president of the Social Service League, that is the League the public would be lead to believe were the promoters of the lecture. And as if to clench the series of inferences he later referred to the lectures of Mr. Budgett eileakin and Mrs. Cranston as the outcome of the Social Service League, all this without stating definitely that the lecture was under the S.S.L. I naturally regarded this as a deliberate attempt on She part of Mr. Dunnico and Mr. ailoorne to work the Social Service League apart from and in spite of its constitutional officials and to circumvent myself as secretary in particular. I had, of course, reason for this, and as Mr. Osborne knew of the tension exist- ing between myself and Mr. Dunnico he ought, I think, in fairness to Messrs. Croetleld, to have prevented the adoption of a course which he must have known would lead to unpleasantness and the implication of gentlemen not in the least to blame. I must refer, although I think most of your readers will already have seen through it, to the absurd contortion of the sense of my letter where I say "nor did I, as secretary, know anything at all about it." None but the very dense could read this in any other light than that as a proof that the lecture was not an EI.S.L. undertaking, 1, as secretary, had nothing to do with the arrangement of it and only became acquainted with it in the same way and at the same time as the general public, that is,by the circular received from Mr. Osborne on the Saturday morning. Following up his consistent assertions that the lecture was not, and was not intended to be, connected with the S.S.L., I notice that Mr. Dunnico intends to try and get the committee "to repudiate me and my letter," and I presume formally adopt the lecture as its own. Well, this is a rather awful prospect for me no doubt, but it has a compensating feature. It opens up a vast avenue of "usefulness" for the League; for if by a resolution the League can father any Project which took place two or three weeks ago and with which the League had nothing to do, then it can do the same with anything that took place two or three months ago, or two or three years or two or three centuries ago, and so by a resole. tion approving of the project of Christopher Columbus, we may say that the discovery of America was made under the auspices of the Social Service League. Then will people inquire "And who's the President of this wonderful League F nd who the Financial Secretary ? "—Yours truly, JOHN MORTON, (sscretuy Social Service League. Watrirgtou,,November 2nd, 1905.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <p>2. What does it contain ?</p>
        <p>3. What is its action ?</p>
        <p>AN EXPLANATORY CATECHISM. ----</p>
        <p>1. What i. Gmilyns titans' Quinine Bitters ?</p>
        <p>An excellent vegetable Remedy, prepared in the most skilful and scientific manner, and containing the active principles of those plants generally acknowledged to be the most remedial agents, and to possess the most heal- ing and curative properties. It is now extensively used in many countries, and everywhere acknowledged as the Best Tonic Remedy known.</p>
        <p>Quinine and the active principles of the following well-known herbs : Sarsaparilla, Saffron, Gentian, Lavender, Burdock, and Dandelion, scientifically pre- pared, and in each happy proportions as to form a Tonic Bitters positively unequalled.</p>
        <p>It assists and promotes Digestion strengthens the Nerves and Muscles, purifies the Blood enlivens the Spirits, and removes all Obstructions and Impurities in the Human Body. It gives tone to the whole system. It strengthens and fortifies those parts which have been weakened by Disease, and are consequently more liable to Colds and unhealthful influences. It has gained the Highest Recommendations as the Best Family Medicine and a remedy for every kind of Weakness.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>Mr. W. Peter Rylands and the Government.</head>
        <p>Mr. W. Peter Rylands and the Government.</p>
        <p>Sir,—lt is so seldom we find anyone courageous enough to defend the present Government that the speech of tr W. P. Hylands at the Conservative Club comes as a startling novelty at the present time. I don't think it could have been better done, considering the material he had to work with. He accounts for the recent reverses the Government has met with by the "swing of pendutum " theory but he thinks it is "diffi- cult to give a logical explanation " of it. I would suggest that the explanation can be found in the follow- ing facts :—That the Government has nearly doubled our National expenditure, enormously increased our National indebtedness. it has piled up the burden of taxation upon the food of the people, thus throwing a dead weight on the trade and commerce of,the country ; reducing the value of Consuls—(our best National security), restricting the incomes of the middle classes, pulling down the purchasing power of the wages of the workers, and thereby narrowing the area of trade and throwing thousands of people out of employment. Home legislation has been neglected. The best and most able men have withdrawn from the Government, their places being filled by novices; with the result that every one of the Government departments is in a state of chaos. _</p>
        <p>We have been plunged into a war which has cost over 200 millions of money and a loss of over 20,000 brave men in order to hand over the enormous mineral wealth of South Africa and the following "British Patriots." R. W. Schumacher E, Birkenruth C. Rube S. Neumann Julius Friedlander L. Wagner L Reyershach Camillo Cerruti</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>If. Strakosch</head>
        <p>If. Strakosch</p>
        <p>NfarqUis d'ilautpoul H. Zeoppritz P. Dreyfus A. Goerz.. A. Iteyersbach J. Freudenthal</p>
        <p>W. llettelbach F. Fr;nools L. Sutro P. A. Molteno</p>
        <p>Arnold Friedlander Anton Dunkels</p>
        <p>Julius Weil</p>
        <p>H Graumann</p>
        <p>F. Voeklein</p>
        <p>Cte. de Ferrtereks T: M. Prillevitz</p>
        <p>A.LambrechN</p>
        <p>M. A. Braunstein B. Kitzinger</p>
        <p>The Government bas provided these gentlemen with Chinamen at one shilling per day to take the place of British workmen, making it necessary for the present Colonial Secretary to warn our working people not to go to South Africa, if they are looking for employ- ment. In fact the motto of the Government seems to be "No English nerd apply." Surely this is enougl. to account for the "swirl,' of the pendulum." Mr. Bylands thinks that no Government could be in office so long without taking a "single false step" but on the whole he says the Conservative party deserve well of the country. Tt is a good thing for us all that the country does not realize its obligations to this Government.</p>
        <p>_ In home politics the legislation—Mr. Rylands admits —has not been " heroic" or great in amount but this he says is the result of the a grossly unfair and obtrusive tactics of the Opposition." This kind of talk is extremely cowardly. Fancy this great Government, with its large majority, and its subservient House of Lords unable to do its work because of a handful of opponents. How wonderfully powerful the Liberal party must be when with their small minority they can paralyse the energies of this great Government ; especially when it is remembered that legislation had been carried by the gag. Discussion has been pre- vented. The Opposition had been closure(' and wrien the Liberals thought they had an opportunity of calling the attention ot the House to the way in which the Government was playing with the questions of Protec- tion and Retaliation the Tory party ran away from the debate sooner than " face the music."</p>
        <p>Mr. Rylands speaks of the Employers' Liability Act as securing compensation for all injuries received by men in the course of their employment. He certainly must know that this is untrue. There are many injuries which this Act makes no provision for and no disabled worker can receive one penny under the legislation during the first two weeks of his disablement. Why did not Mr. Rylands tell his aud;ence that this measure, such as it is, was ca,ried by Liberal votes ? Does he not know that on the night of the division on the second reading a large number of the Tories refused to vote for the measure, and that the Liberals in a body went into the Tory lobby and saved the bill ; so that every penny the worker gets under it he has to thank the Liberals for. The Education Act Mr. Rylands thinks is very good, despite the fact that it has thrown a large and import- ant section of the community into open revolt against the law, Representative Educational Institutions have been destroyed. The people's schools, built with the people's money have been taken from popular control. Respectable citizens, men of intelligence and integrity are compelled to appear in the police court to resist this invasion of their conscience, and once again the goods of the Protestant Dissenter are being sold by public auction to satisfy the thirst for power of Mother Church.</p>
        <p>"Mr. Rylands also eulogises the Licensing Act. He does not speak of it as a brewer's bill, which it undoubt- edly is, for the tenant in many cases will have to pay, and not receive ; while the brewers will make a very good thing out of it. The nation must remember that this Act—for the first time—creates a vested interest in the [liquor Trade, which is one of the most dangerous things that has been done for many years. Mr. Rylands' references to Protection are so fallacious that it is quite impossible for me to do them justice in this letter, therefore I think I had better let Mr. Chamberlain answer him with the following extract from one of his speeches "I think it necessary that we should once more rub up our history of those bad times before Mr. Bright and Mr. Cobden succeeded in persuading Parliament that the Corn Laws were an iniquitous tax. I wonder whether in this vast audience there are any people who have any conception of the state of things which existed forty and fifty years ago (1835-15) ! At that time the whole of the labour- ers in the agricultural districts were on the verge of starvation. The poor rates in some districts were 20s, in the pound. At the time of which I am speaking the large towns were described by eye- witnesses as bearing the appearance of beleaguredi cities, so dreadful was the destitution and misery which prevailed in them. People walked the street like gaunt shadows and not like human beings. There were bread riots in almost every town. There were rick burnings on all the country sides. There was famine throughout the length and breadth of Ireland. There was only one class that profited, and that was the landlords, who continued to exact their rents. We were on the verge of a revolution when the Corn Laws were abolished. You know that T have recently preached that all is not well with us even yet—that there is room for improve- ment in the great mass of the people. But that condition whatever it might be—the depression of trade, the lack of employment—is as nothing beside the misery and destitution which prevailed when the Corn Laws were in full force. It is well to remember these things. If we are going back to this time let us do it with our eyes open." Mr. Chamberlain said this at Birmingham in November 1835. He has since changed, but the facts have not. Apologising for troubling you at such length, I re- main yours, Morecambe.</p>
        <p>J. HOWES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>DANGERS FROM COLDS &amp; CHILLS.</head>
        <p>DANGERS FROM COLDS &amp; CHILLS.</p>
        <p>How Bile Beans Benefited a Durham</p>
        <p>Miner.</p>
        <p>In the British Isles something like 200,000 persons die annually, either directly or indirectly from colds and chills. If your system is weak, you are the less able to withstand attacks from cold. Bile Beans , strengthen weak systems, and assist in warding off colds and their effects. In a letter to the Bileßean Co. Mr. Harry Cook Stangroom, Bear Park Colliery,Durhara describes how a cold produced acute rheumatism, "Through working:in wet, staamsjl contracted a cold which eventually terminated in an attack of rheu- matism," he states. "I awoke one morning with severe pains in the ankle, and I had to creep downstairs as best I could, the pain meanwhile being agonising. I saw a doctor, who thought I had received a blow or had sprained my ankle. Gradually the pains increased, and I was utterly unable to do anything for myself. and bad to be lifted off and on the bed. My appetito completely failed, and the little food I managed to take was not sufficient to feed a child, I sank so rapidly that the neighbours thought I should die. "Hearing of Bile Beans, I decided to try them. By the time I had finished the first box I could eat as well as ever, and the rheumatism was rapidly leaving me. I started work again, and soon felt my old vigourreturn, Now thanks entirely to Bile Beans, I am quite cured." Bile Beans, besides warding off colds,chills, neuralgia, and influenza, are a permanent cure for biliousness, In- digestion, constipation, piles, ansemis " nerves" female ailments, weakness, dizziness, rheumatism, pains in the back and side, insomnia, loss of appetite, congested liver, headache, flatulence, pimples and other eruptions and ailments having a common origin in impaired action of stomach and liver. Of all chemists, or post free from the Bile Bean Manufacturing Co., Red Cross Street, London, E.C., on receipt of prices, Is. 11.,c1. or 2s. 9d. (2s. 9d. contains three times the Is. ild. size).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>Mr. R. Cameron, M.P. at Sankey Bridges.</head>
        <p>Mr. R. Cameron, M.P. at Sankey Bridges.</p>
        <p>Mr, Robert Cameron, M.P., preached at special services held at Sankey Brides Wesleyan Church on Sunday with the object of raising a fund to carry on mission work in the district. His eloquent discourses were listened to very attentively by large congregations his text in the morning being "Jesus wept and in the evening" The prodigal son." Miss Constance M. Eaton, of Oakeixgates, Shropshire, Sang solos very sweetly at each service. Mr. E. G. Rickers was at the organ. In the afternoon a musical service was held in the schoolroom, the Rev. A. Cecil Parker pre- siding. An enjoyable programme was provided by the Juvenile Harmonic Choir under the conductorship of Mr. T. Brough.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <p>u estion. He knew nothing about the two members. Councillor PEMBERTON : I don't withdraw a word have said.</p>
        <p>Alderman TENSION: We don't expect you to with- draw anything. Councillor PEMBERTON : No; not when it is true. TheMavort: I think as an interested party you should say nothing, Mr. Pemberton. Councillor PEMBERTON : SO is Alderman Tinnion You treat me most unfairly. You allow my accusers a hearing, but you don't allow the accused. The MAYOR : There's nobody who has accused you. Councillor PEMBERTON : You do ; you say I am interested. That is twice you have said it in this Council Chamber. How do you know? The YIA YOB. : Well, according to the report sent in to the General Purposes Committee. If you are not interested who is ?</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON : Haven't las much right to speak as toe men who bring these accusations before you? The 111.tvou : You wAre there at the Investigation and heard it all.</p>
        <p>Councillor PENIBEIRTON : No, I was not. Now, Councillor Clegg, will you give me the name of the person you inferred gave me the information, and what were the circumstances which you say I know of and ought not to know? Mr. Mayor, I ask Councillor Clegg through you as to the statement he makes. The MAYOR. : I impress upon you to discuss this in a fair manner.</p>
        <p>COUBCMOr PEMBERTON : lam trying to do. The MAYOR : Take it on its merits. Don't bring in any petty spleen and that sort of business. Councillor PEnuEnToN : Am I bringing in petty spleen ? The MAYOR : I don't know what you are bringing in, but you are sailing as near the wind as possible. Councillor PEMBERTON : Mr. Clegg has made an accusation, surely I have a right to know what it is. Councillor CLEGG : I shall be very pleased to let him know. Councillor Pemberton stated in this chamber a month ago that Alderman Pierpoint never suggested putting colouring to this matter. I say Alderman Pier- point suggested that colouring should be put into it. I say Councillor Pemberton has no right to know that because it was a secret to the committee. There's the answer.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON : It turns out as usual to be all moonshine. (Laughter.) The same as all Councillor Clegg's speeches are. What I say—and it is in the Press—is that the accusation was made against Alder- man Pierpoint at the previous meeting of this Council and not at the Investigation Committee, Fancy Coun- cillor Clegg coming here interfering in a serious matte like this -when he does not know the common rudiments of it and his memory won't carry him back to what took place at the last Council meeting. How dare he come here and say that. It is a serious matter. It is a criminal matter, where a man may get eighteen months. It shows Councillor Clegg's fairness. it shows what anybody can expect from him. I should not like to be the poor beggar to be tried by Councillor Clegg. He's a beauty. The MAYOR : This is not doing you any good. It is only a case of the pot calling the kettle. Councillor PEMBERTON : The difference is that what I Councillor Clegg says is untrue, and what I say to you are facts. The Maxon: Why call one another and sav they are untrue? Can't you speak without bringing all this in? 1 Councillor PEMBERTON: No, sir. When a man gets up and makes a statement which is totally untrue whether it is wilful or accidental I cannot prove my promises without saying he is telling an untruth. The MAYOR: It is not parliamentary to tell a mem- ber he is telling untruths. Conncillor CLEGG: lam quite content to let it rest with the Council. It is, impossible for a man like Councillor Pemberton to blemish my character. Councillor PEMBERTON: I believe you are the "lick spittler" of the officials. Councillor PEMBERTON said with regard to Alderman Tinnion's remarks it was not a question whether the Town Clerks should be trusted or not, and Alderman Tinnion's reference to the Town Clerk as public prose- cutor was not on all fours with this case for this reason. If the Town Clerk acted in the ordinary way as public prosecutor, he acted from information supplied by the police. This was a different case altogether. It had been stated that the Deputy Town Clerk drew up the case, and submitted it to the Investigation Com- mittee. There was no objection to that, and on the advice of the barrister, the committee recommended what they did. Councillor Clegg and a few others seemed to think that they should rule the committee. As a matter of fact he found there were a few other gentlemen on the committee besides them, and the majority must have agreed to a certain course of action being taken. The point he wished to mention was that the Town Clerk would not yet take his positiona4publio prosecutor in the ordinary way. lie would do that when the Attorney-General granted permission for the prosecu- tion to take place. In the meantime the Town Clerk was the servant of the committee, and as the Town Clerk was a deeply interested party in one of those charges, was it fair that he should have the drawing up solely to himself? What could the committee do if the Town Clerk drew his case up and submitted it to the committee? They could not alter- anything without the consent of the Council. Therefore there could be no possible objection to the Town Clerk submitting the case to the investigation Committee or the membrs of the Council. The chief point was that they must see that the whole of the evidence, and not part of it, should go before the Attorney-General. The point was would the whole of the evidence go before the Attorney- General? The MAYOR : It is bound to go. Councillor PEMBERTON : Is it ? The Maxon : Yes. Councillor PEMBERTON : Will you guarantee it ? The MAYOR : No ; but as far as I know it is bound to go and everything will have to be got in order. The affidavits have got to be made. Councillor PEMBERTON : Had you seen it ? The MAYOR : Onlyas a member of the committee. Councillor PEMBERTON: You know in your evidence as a magistrate that a lot of this is not evidence. The MAYOR : I know it is. Councillor PEMBERTON : But that won't go before the Attorney-General, will it ? What the Torn Coun- cillors want, I understand, and what I want and what -ought to be is that the whole of the case should go before the Attorney-General. The TOWN CLERK : I will take good care it does. Councillor PEMBERTON: Then there can be no objec- tion to the Investigation Committee seeing it. The TOWN CLERK : I object to you seeing my brief for the prosecution. Councillor PEMBERTON : I don't want to see it. I never asked for it. When did I ask to Lee the brief? The TOWN CLERK: That is what you want. Councillor PEMBERTON : I defy any man in Warring- ton or you either to prove what you say. It is not the first untruth you have told, you know. The MAYOR: I say, cannot you discuss it as gentle- men? Comicillor PENtemroN ; You allowed the Town Clerk to say I wanted to see his brief. You didn't pull him up. I say it is a deliberate lie. The MAYOR: You dent need to say it in that way. Cannot you gloss it over and put it in better form ? Councillor PEMBERTON : No ; that is plain English that is. Considering the serionsness of the caseand the dastardly way in which it has been worked no other language would fit the case. The MAYOR : Yes ; but there is an old saving that the man who makes his language that strong and his oaths that big, is generally over-shooting the mark. Councillor PEMBERTON : 011,1 could put it stronger. The MAYOR : It would not be believed. Councillor PEMBERTON : I flatter myself that my word is as good in the town as the Town Clerk's though you might not think it. There are certain people in this Town Council who simply lick the boots of the Town Clerk. In justice I demand that the Coun- cil should see that the whole of the case is put before the Attorney-General. Ihe TOWN CLERK : It will be. Councillor PEMBERTON : I know what your promises are.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: You know this is only spleen. Councillor PEMBERTON: It is not spleen. There are a few people in the Council to whom whatever the Town Clerk said would be all right. There can be no mistake about the newspapers. They don't put things down that are not true. You see Alderman Pierpoint is so disgusted that he has left the meeting. He had a distinct promise from the Town Clerk that the case should be submitted to the committee. There is no doubt about it there was a majority of this Town Coun- cil thought we were promised to see the statement of the cases. Justice can in no possible way suffer from the committee or if you like the Council seeing the evidence the rown Clerk is going to place bofore the Attorney-General. It cannot affect anything. I would like while I am on my feet to say this : In the Warrington Press last week but one there appeared a parapaph stating that additional evidence had been obtained in one of the cases in this investigation. I would like to know who communicated that to the Press and what was it communicated for. The MAYOR: I don't know. _ _</p>
        <p>Councillor Dr. JOSEPH : There is one remark I don't understand andl would like it answered through you Mr. Mayor. Councillor Pemberton said that the Town Clerk was interested deeply interested, in this matter. I do not understand that.</p>
        <p>PEMBERTON: I will give the explanation. [t is this, that the concloqion that has been come to by counsel and the committee on the advice of counsel had been come to on the evidence or part evidence of the Town Clerk who gave evidence as a witness. That is where I say ho is interested. The TowN Cr.narc: All I related to the committee was a statement made to me by Mr. James Lawson, I am in no sense interested,_</p>
        <p>Councillor BunzoNT: What is the object of this dis• cussion ? Will the motion be put to the Council? The MAYOR: The Town Clerk tells me not. Councillor BURTON: Well, what is the use of going on and wasting time. The AlAvon, : I have allowed you to discuss it ad lib. It will simply fill up the newspapers who make a construction of their own which is generally off the mark as far as it possibly oln be. if asked my opinion of this thing, I should not like to say, because I might offend somebody. We came here with honest intentions to conduct the Council business for benefit of the com- munity but this sort of business is one pulling against another.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <p>Councillor LORD : I rise to a point of order. Haver I a right to reply? The NIA.voR : There is nothing before us. Alderman Tinnion asked me and I said I would stay here while you discussed this. It was only a matter of courtesy. Councillor LORD: You have allowed it to be pro- posed, seconded end discussed. The MAYOR: This is out of order.</p>
        <p>Councillor LORD : Do you rule I have no right to reply. The MAYOR : Yes, the whole thing is out of order. It is an informal discussion. You wanted to air your grievances. You simply wanted to talk and I have allowed you to do so. Councillor LORD: Well, I want to talk, Mr. Mayor. Does the Town Clerk look upon it as infra dig that he should submit the case to us?</p>
        <p>The Al kYOR : I have never asked him. The TowN CLERK : T think it is most improper and most unprofessional. It is infra dig, if you like. Councillor LORD asked why, if the Town Clerk was taking up this position now, did he allow the Deputy Town Clerk to draw up the cases and lay them before the committee before they went before the Council. The I\avon. said that was not the proper place to raise the question. Councillor LORD : It has been asked whether we are prepared to trust the Town Clerk. The 111 Avon. : There are always some members who trust nobody. Councillor LORD : It is not a question of trusting the Town Clerk. None of us are infallible. All that we wanted is that the whole of the facts would be placed before the Attorney-General, and we have that assurance for the first time.</p>
        <p>The Tows CLERK : Nis- case will be quite complete. Counoillor LO?tD : Will a copy of the evidence and printed proceedings be sent to the Attorney-General ? The TOWN CLERK : No, it is not admissible—not ,the whole of it. Depend upon it the case will be com- plete. Councillor LORD : It has been stated that the Town Clerk is an interested person. The Tows CLERK : How can I be?</p>
        <p>Councillor LORD : There is a rumour that other pro- ceedings will be taken, and if those proceedings are begun, the Town Clerk will be an interested person and very interested too. The NIA.YOIL : lam tired of these investigations and proceedings. Councillor LORD : Well, you do as much talking as all the rest of the Council put together. If the matter is out of order I will let it go. Councillor BARTON thought the Investigation commit- tee should have been able to show a better result. It reflected upon the Council's selection and indeed upon the Council as a whole if the matter was to come to nothing. Ile hoped that the matter would be thoroughly investigated so that everything would be fair in every shape and form. Councillor CLEGG : I have endeavoured to be fair and unbiassed but I have found on that Investigation Committee men who have determined not to be fair. (Cries of" Oh.") Councillor Lour) : I object to that. Alderman SMETuURST : you may object. The NIAXOR : Are you going to let the cap fit,whother it does or not? Councillor CLEGG : I would not stoop to some of the tricks that have been practised in reference to this matter. We have had members of the Investigation Committee meeting ma side street with parties who are charged. I don't know what the conversation has been. lam actually told that the Chairman of the Committee has had conferences with one of the parties concerned. I have heed anxious all the way through that the evidence as it was taken should be submitted. Councillor LORD: That is all we want.</p>
        <p>Councillor C LEG G : That is all I want but when the members of the committee areanxious to put colouring in, what does it mean ? You must take good care, too, that no reporter was present when it was said. I was- present— Councillor LORD ; Who kept the reporter out ? Councillor CLEGG : I have nothing to do with that. No reporter was present. I did not keep him out. Councillor LORD : Did you suggest that ho should come in ?</p>
        <p>Councillor CLEGG : The reporter wa.s asked to leave the room by the Chairman of the committee. Councillor LORD : With the consent of the committee. Councillor CLEGG : I have tried to be fair to all concerned and lam determined that I shall be fair. I will not stoop either to one party or the other. All I am anxious for is that the evidence shall be placed before the Attorney-General as it is. What good is it to me that any member of the Council shall suffer? I say that by the bias of some members of the committee members of the Council will suffer. They have damaged their cause.</p>
        <p>Alderman SMETHURST : A good deal. Councillor CLEGG t sin anxious that the Council should prosecute, because I don't think any member of the Council should have to go to the trouble to prosecute on his own account. I don't say the cases are good—l leave that to other judges. It is right that we shall present the case to the Attorney-General as it is, and if he advises proceedings, let us proceed. It is the honour of the Council that we seek to maintain. Officialism has nothing to do with it as far as I ern concerned. Only be straight. Councillor PEMBERTON I would like to ask whether it is true that already the case is placed before the Attornc*-General ? The TOWN CLERIC replied that it was not, but it would be this week. The discussion then ended.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>SPECIAL COUNCIL MEETING ASKED FOR.</head>
        <p>SPECIAL COUNCIL MEETING ASKED FOR.</p>
        <p>Requisition to the Mayor. As a sequel to the unsatisfactory conclusion of the Council proceedings on Tuesday evening at which Councillor Lord's resolution calling upon the Town Clerk to lay before the Investigation Committee the cases to be submitted to the Attorney General was ruled out of order, the Chairman (Alderman Pierpoint) and four members of the Investigation Committee have sent in a requisition to the Mayor for a special meeting of the Town Council to further consider the question. Seven days' notice of such a meeting has to be given so that it is therefore possible for the meet- ing to be held either before or immediately after the annual meeting of the Council at noon on Thursday next. The Mayor, of course, has the option of com- plying with the requisition but in the event of his refusal to do so, the five members of the Council who have made the request have power to call a meeting. We understand that the two other members of the In- vestigation Committee (Alderman Smethurst and Coun- cillor Clegg) were given the opportunity of signing the requisition but declined. The requisition reads as follows To the Mayor of Warrington, Alderman Bolton, J.P. We, the undersigned five members of the Warrington Town Council do hereby request you to call a meeting of the Town Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>B USINE SS.</head>
        <p>B USINE SS.</p>
        <p>To consider the proceedings of the late Investigation Committee and the proceedings of the Council in relation thereto, and to propose resolutions thereon. WM. PIERPOINT, JAS. G. CRATE, FRED STOTT. JOHN LORD, THOS. H. ASHCROFT. The application to the Attorney-General has not yet been made, but we understand that the papers necessary to the preparation of the case have been sent to London.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>DROPSY, BRIGHT'S DISEASE, AND HEART WEAKNESS. •</head>
        <p>DROPSY, BRIGHT'S DISEASE, AND HEART WEAKNESS. •</p>
        <p>Unable to Work for 32 Weeks.</p>
        <p>CURED BY VEND'S SEAWEED TONIC.</p>
        <p>Mr. JOHN MACMILLAN, 15, Commercial Street. Bridgetvl, Perth, writes, June 30th, 1905 :—" At the beginning of June, 190 i, I got a severe chill on the kidneys, and after a long attendance of two doctors, I was told it was Bright's disease. I had also groat weak, ness of the heart, and suffered terribly from dropsy. When the first doctor stopped coming, we sent for another, but he had not been attending me long, when he said there was not much hope; so I stopped his treatment and took VENO'S SEAWEED TONIC. That was in February last ; since then it has done for me what two doctors did not do in nearly a year. I was off work 32 weeks, but I am now able to (maim/a my work daily, and I am about as well as ever I was except for a little swelling in the legs." VENO'S SEAWEED TONIC for purity and all-round potency has not its equal anywhere for kidney troubles, stomach, liver and blood diseases ; it strengthens, regulates, and restores the body to a healthy and vigorous state. Especially good for chronic constipa- tion, indigestion and all kidney and heart affections. Price is. Id. and 2s. 9d. at Chemists everywhere.</p>
