        <p>Bate initiated a passing movement, which led up to RUGBY FOOTBALL. Berry increasing the score, but the kick at goal again proved a failure. A breakaway by the visitors resulted ' in the game being taken near the home line,but a capital dribble, by the home forwards witnessed the game in the visitor's quarters once more, where Berry succeeded in scoring. Another passing exhibition ended in Isher- wood scoring while just in time one of the forwards scrambled over. A one-sided contest resnited in a victory for Walker's by one goal seven tries (23 points), to Aspull, nil. 1 r STOCKTON HEATH V. CHEADLE HULME " A."— • Stockton Heath played their first home match for the season on Saturda3 when they entertained Cheadle RESULTS OF SATURDAY'S MATCHES. Hulrne second team on the Ellesmere-road ground. The NORTHERN LEAGUE, home team won the toss and in the first half G. T. P. G. T. p had alslight assistance from theiwind. Play ruled even Swinton -0 2 Runcorn O-0 0 for some time when Stockton Heath tock up a strong Leeds -4 16 Rochdale H....... 0-1 3 position in the visitors quarters. A forward dribble Dewsbury 3-4 18 Broughton R. ... 0-0 0 carried the ball over the line where Daintith picked it Huddersfield -7 23 Normanton ...... 0-1 3 up and scored. K. Gibbs majorised. After this reverse Cheadle Hulme worked hard and took up the attack' but LANCASHIRE SENIOR CUP.— SECOND ROUND. the defence prevailed and half time arrived with the Wigan -0 4 Broughton R. ... 0-1 3 score unaltered. Re-starting Stockton Heath at once Oldham 2-2 10 Salford -0 0 attacked and keeping up the pressure Bradshaw scored Leigh l-1 5 Warrington ...... 0-0 2 from a scrimmage near the line. Cheadle linlme were Widnes O-3 9 Barrow -0 0 unable to make any headway and Stockton Heath ran YORKSHIRE SENIOR CUP.—SECOND ROUND. out winners by 8 points to nil. Hunslet B-7 37 Keighley -1 3 Halifax 2-2 10 Hull -0 0 York l-0 2 Bradford -0 0 Batley -1 5 Hull K.R l-1 5 Warrington Lose to Leigh. For the first time in the history of the meetings of the two clubs Warrington were beaten by Leigh in a cup-tie on Saturday. The match took place at Warring- ton and was witnessed by 7,000 people. Warrington were still handicapped by the absence of Fish and Dickenson from the three-quarter line, while Leigh were at full strength. Teams and officials : Warrington—Hallam, back ; Harris, Isherwood, Jordan, and Kenyon, three-quarter backs ; Hockenhall and Brooks, half backs ; Preston, Boardman, Thomas, Cook. Jolley, Swift, Harmer and Belton, forwards. Leigh—Clarkson, back; Neville, Bennett, T. Johnson, and S. Johnson, three-punter hacks; Whittaker and Dunbavin, half backs ; Bury. O'Brien, Lee, O'Neil, Roberts, Silcock, Webster, and Winstanley, for ;cards. Referee, 3.1 r. F. Renton, Hunslet ; touch judges, Messrs. G. Wightman (Swinton), and J. Harrison .(Chadderton). Leigh set the ball in motion and at once assumed the offensive. Smart forward work and good kicking by Dunbavin and Whittaker carried play to the home line. Hockenhall and Brooks gained temporary relief, but the Leighinen would not be denied and again attached hotly, Dunbavin nearlrdodging over. A brilliant clear- ance by Hallam was cheered to the echo, but Bennett and Whittaker quickly ran the ball back to the home quarter. From a scrummage close in, Whittaker picked upand coolly dropped a goal. The Warrington forwards next asserted themselves and a splendid dribble headed by Thomas took the ball over the Leigh line, Isherwood grounding when off-side and spoiling the effort. From this point to the interval the visitors continued to have slightly the best of matters, but rarely looked like scoring. Preston had three attempts at goal-kicking he failed each time. Half-time Leigh 1 goal (2 points); Warrington nil. Preston re-started, and a long kick sent Dunbavin in trouble on the Leigh line. Warrington displayed rare dash, Thomas and Boardman nearly facing their way over. The forwards quite overplayed the Leigh eight and some keen play in the visitors quarters was the result. Brooks and Hockenhall thrice got the ball away to Harris on the right wiyig, but he was over- Weln;ed by numbers. Over-eagerness to relieve got Leigh penalised, and this time Preston scored with a fine shot against the wind. The teams were now equal and with the Warrington forwards in such good trim they looked like winning. A sad blunder by Kenyon, however, let his side down. The ball was kicked to the goal-line and S. Johnson beating Hockenhall in the race for possession scored far cut. Dunbavin failed at goal. Warrington again raised hopes with fine forward play but the disorganised state of the back division spoiled many chances. In the last ten minutes,Hocken- hall and Thomas both made strong efforts to cross, but the Leigh men defended well, and finally won a keen game by 5 points to 2 points.</p>
